// AUTO-EXTRACTED verbatim from the pre-rewrite room/ending files — this IS the v1
// English text pack: the original voice, preserved as a selectable "backup".
// Do not hand-edit; regenerate with `npx vite-node scripts/extract-v1.ts` if needed.
import { registerAll } from './resolver';

registerAll('v1', 'en', {
  "ending.dissolved.beat0": `Somewhere between one room and the next, the last of the grip lets go.`,
  "ending.dissolved.beat1": `It is not what you feared. That is the first finding, and you register it with what used to be surprise: no fall, no dark, no teeth. The dissolving is a tide going out — the name first, then the roles, then the story, each one lifting off like a coat you had forgotten you were wearing indoors.`,
  "ending.dissolved.beat2": `For a while, something watches the pieces go, with interest and without alarm. The watching does not have a name anymore. It never needed one; that is the second finding.`,
  "ending.dissolved.beat3": `The rooms take back their files. The wallet, the lever, the folder, the stones — all of it returns to the great cabinet, patiently, to be walked through by whoever dissolves next, and whoever after them. Your choices stay in the rooms like warmth stays in a chair.`,
  "ending.dissolved.beat4": `The Usher sits with what is left until there is no with left to sit. He keeps his word; nobody dissolves alone on his shift. At the end he says something — the tradition requires it — and what he says is: “You were here. I saw you. That part doesn’t dissolve; it just stops being carried by you specifically.”`,
  "ending.dissolved.beat5": `And the tide completes, and the sea — which was always the thing the fog was hiding — receives the rest, and the sea has never once lost a single thing that mattered.`,
  "ending.dissolved.epitaph": `The sea has never once lost a single thing that mattered.`,
  "ending.dissolved.note.body": `Every tradition that maps the self’s territory marks this region: the point where the constructed “I” — name, roles, story, the whole administrative apparatus — comes apart entirely. Mystics court it; psychiatry watches it carefully; the dying sometimes describe arriving at it with unexpected calm. The finding reported back, across traditions that agree on nothing else, is strangely consistent: what dissolves is the packaging, and what panics at the prospect is also the packaging. This ending is reachable from every corridor of the game because it is reachable from every corridor of a life — and it was written as an ending, not a failure screen, on purpose. Not to romanticize the losing of a self, but to record honestly what the rooms taught: that the grip was always borrowed, the carrying always temporary, and the witness — whatever that was — was never on the list of things that could be dropped.`,
  "ending.dissolved.note.thinkers": `ego death · impermanence · what remains`,
  "ending.dissolved.note.title": `On Dissolution`,
  "ending.dissolved.title": `The Dissolved`,
  "ending.fortress.beat0": `You step through, and the threshold closes behind you like a well-machined lid.`,
  "ending.fortress.beat1": `You wake competent. The dissolution is over, categorically: you rebuilt the walls the rooms took down, and you rebuilt them better — the memories reinforced, the opinions load-tested, the perimeter patrolled by a vigilance that never quite clocks out.`,
  "ending.fortress.beat2": `It works. That must be said plainly: it works. You return to the world and the world, which respects a sealed structure, promotes you. Decisions come easy. Grief bounces. The unfinished arguments get finished, on your terms, efficiently.`,
  "ending.fortress.beat3": `People describe you with words like solid, together, unshakeable, and they are correct, and you notice — with a small flat feeling you file under miscellaneous — that no one has described you as warm in some time.`,
  "ending.fortress.beat4": `The fortress keeps everything out. That is what fortresses are for. It is years before you stand at a window one evening and let yourself complete the thought: everything out. The weather, the wolves, the noise — and the mail, and the visitors, and whatever it was that used to get in back when you leaked.`,
  "ending.fortress.beat5": `Somewhere, on an odd day, the Usher files your card with a small sigh. “Safe,” he writes, in the outcome field. And then, because the form has no box for it, in the margin: “Safe from what, exactly, was never specified.”`,
  "ending.fortress.epitaph": `Nothing got in. That was the plan. Nothing got in.`,
  "ending.fortress.note.body": `The Stoics built the original inner citadel: make your judgments the only thing you value, and no fortune can breach you. It is a real technology and it genuinely works — that is its danger. Psychology finds the same structure in defended selves: armor laid down against a real threat, kept on long after the war, until protection and imprisonment share a wall. Rilke’s warning was exact: “our deepest fears are like dragons guarding our deepest treasure” — seal the vault against the dragons and the treasure seals too. A self that nothing can wound is also a self nothing can astonish. The fortress ending is not a failure; it is a trade, honestly priced. The sting is only this: the door locks from the inside, and the keyholder is the one person who never checks whether the siege ended.`,
  "ending.fortress.note.thinkers": `stoic fortification · Rilke · defended selves`,
  "ending.fortress.note.title": `On Armor`,
  "ending.fortress.title": `The Fortress`,
  "ending.gardener.beat0": `You take the clipboard. It is warm, and it has your name on it — legible at last, which tells you something about what names are for: they are for shifts.`,
  "ending.gardener.beat1": `The Usher shows you the facility the way an old gardener shows a garden: not the map, the temperament. Which rooms run cold. Which door sticks in autumn, insofar as there is autumn. How the trolley likes its tracks greased and the casino likes its felt brushed and the small polite room likes, above all, to be left alone.`,
  "ending.gardener.beat2": `Travelers come. Dissolved, frightened, certain, each one convinced their wallet, their fire, their folder is the first of its kind. You learn the keeper’s discipline: you may polish the lever, but never pull it for them. You may light the corridor, but never name the door. There are no answers to withhold — that part turns out to be true — but there is company to keep, and company, kept properly, is most of what the rooms ever dispensed.`,
  "ending.gardener.beat3": `You get good at it. Centuries are excellent for craft. Sometimes a traveler notices the trap in the courtroom, and you press one chip back into their hand for luck. Sometimes one sits down among the stones and refuses, and you bring the coat, and say the thing about the current, and mean it a little more each era.`,
  "ending.gardener.beat4": `And one day — Bronze Age of some future accounting — a traveler at the threshold turns from the morning, looks at you with recognition you remember from the inside, and holds out a hand for the clipboard.`,
  "ending.gardener.beat5": `You give it over. It is warm. It has their name on it, suddenly legible. “First lesson,” you hear yourself say, in a voice with a flicker in it, “the halo and the horns are the same size. It’s on purpose. Everything here is.” And you step through your threshold at last — the one that was, of course, behind the reception desk the entire time.`,
  "ending.gardener.epitaph": `The rooms always need a keeper. The keeper always needed the rooms.`,
  "ending.gardener.note.body": `Voltaire ended Candide — his catalogue of the world’s absurd cruelties — with the most anticlimactic wisdom in literature: we must cultivate our garden. Not solve the world; tend a corner of it. The Gardener ending is the absurd hero’s second act: Camus’s Sisyphus, having claimed the boulder, discovers there are other people on the hill and that the rock rolls easier with company watching. The wounded-healer archetype (Chiron, and every sponsor, therapist, and veteran guide since) holds that the deepest qualification for tending others through a passage is having been dismantled by it yourself. Staying was not refusing the return. It was returning — to the only reality this place has — and choosing to be furniture in it: the good kind, the kind a lost traveler can lean on. Somebody polished that lever for you, once. Now you know their name.`,
  "ending.gardener.note.thinkers": `Camus · Voltaire, “il faut cultiver notre jardin” · the wounded healer`,
  "ending.gardener.note.title": `On Tending`,
  "ending.gardener.title": `The Gardener`,
  "ending.open-hand.beat0": `You step through, and the world receives you the way water receives — no seam, no splash.`,
  "ending.open-hand.beat1": `You wake porous. That is the only word for it. The membrane between you and everyone else came back from the rooms thinner, and it never quite thickens again.`,
  "ending.open-hand.beat2": `You feel the checkout clerk’s exhaustion in your own shoulders. You cry at other people’s reunions in airports. Strangers tell you things on trains — they can smell the open door on you — and you carry every confession home.`,
  "ending.open-hand.beat3": `You become, quietly, the person others are repaired by. It is a good life, measured in other people’s better days. The ledger of it is kept in a currency you can no longer count, because counting was one of the walls that dissolved.`,
  "ending.open-hand.beat4": `Only sometimes — late, in the honest hours — do you notice what the open hand cannot do, which is close. You gave the rooms your armor and they kept it. Whether that was the price or the prize is a question you leave, deliberately, unanswered on the table each night.`,
  "ending.open-hand.beat5": `And the Usher, if he could see you — and on odd days, who knows — would say: we returned that one lighter. We may have removed slightly more packaging than the regulations strictly advise.`,
  "ending.open-hand.epitaph": `You gave yourself away, coin by coin, gladly — mostly gladly.`,
  "ending.open-hand.note.body": `Simone Weil held that attention — pure, unselfed attention to another — is the rarest and purest form of generosity, and she practiced it to the point of self-erasure, dying at 34 having refused to eat more than the rationed people she stood with. The traditions that prize boundless compassion all carry the same fine print: the bodhisattva who vows to save all beings must somehow remain someone who can act, and a boundary is not the opposite of love — it is love’s load-bearing wall. An open hand that cannot close cannot carry. The rooms rewarded your openness; the world will too, and it will also, without malice, bill it. Porousness is a gift with a maintenance schedule.`,
  "ending.open-hand.note.thinkers": `Simone Weil · the bodhisattva problem`,
  "ending.open-hand.note.title": `On Boundless Compassion`,
  "ending.open-hand.title": `The Open Hand`,
  "ending.punchline.beat0": `The small door opens on a small room, and the small room contains: the Usher, already seated, pouring two cups of the thing from the casino that steams. There is no third chair. There was never a need.`,
  "ending.punchline.beat1": `USHER: You noticed the courtroom trap. You kept your lucidity through the fog. And you heard the laughing when the others heard a draft. So — regulations, at this point, actually require me to do this. I’ve checked. I wrote the regulations.`,
  "ending.punchline.beat2": `He removes the halo. Under it: nothing unusual. He removes the horns. Under them: likewise. And then — with the practiced care of someone taking off a very old coat — he removes the shadow itself.`,
  "ending.punchline.beat3": `And there is no one in the chair. There was never anyone in the chair. The voice, when it continues, comes from exactly where it has been coming from the entire journey — a location you suddenly, roaringly, recognize, because you have been hearing it since long before the waiting room, in the pause before every choice you ever made.`,
  "ending.punchline.beat4": `USHER: God on odd days, the Devil on even days — and you never asked who was keeping the calendar. There was never anyone here but you, traveler. The rooms were yours. The trolley was yours. The kind lie and the wallet and the child in the basement — yours, staged nightly, in the only theater that was ever in operation. I’m the bit of you that asks. You’re the bit that answers. We’ve been doing this show a very long time, and tonight — finally — the audience got the joke.`,
  "ending.punchline.beat5": `And you laugh. It starts small and then it doesn’t stop and it doesn’t need to — the laugh of the punchline that was sitting in plain sight for twenty rooms, for a whole life, for however long the fog was up. You laugh, and the facility laughs — same voice — and the fog, having no further function, closes like a curtain call.`,
  "ending.punchline.beat6": `You wake laughing. An actual morning, an actual bed, tears of it still on your face and the joke already evaporating the way the great ones do — leaving only its shape, which is this: there was never a way back to reality. You never left. There was only, ever, the one of you, asking itself the whole way home.`,
  "ending.punchline.epitaph": `There was never anyone here but you. Good joke, isn’t it?`,
  "ending.punchline.note.body": `The Upanishads compress it to three words: tat tvam asi — thou art that. The seeker and the sought, the questioner and the questioned, the traveler and the facility: one thing, playing hide-and-seek with itself and playing to win. Alan Watts spent a career retelling it as comedy, because comedy is the only genre with the right timing: the punchline works precisely because it was visible all along — every mystical tradition’s final secret is an open one, guarded not by locks but by the seeker’s certainty that it must be elsewhere. The laughing, the traditions agree, is diagnostic: enlightenment-as-tragedy is usually misfiled melancholy, but the real recognition arrives as a laugh, because the distance you spent everything crossing turns out never to have existed. God on odd days, the Devil on even days, and you keeping the calendar the entire time. It took you twenty rooms. The average, the Usher would want noted, is considerably worse.`,
  "ending.punchline.note.thinkers": `tat tvam asi · Alan Watts · the cosmic joke`,
  "ending.punchline.note.title": `The Oldest Joke`,
  "ending.punchline.title": `The Punchline`,
  "ending.return.beat0": `You step through, and the threshold does the one thing no room could: it ends.`,
  "ending.return.beat1": `You wake somewhere plausible — a bed, a chair, a patch of grass outside a building you recognize — with morning underway and your name arriving a half-second later, on time, fitting like a key that has been cut slightly better.`,
  "ending.return.beat2": `The world has not improved. Traffic is traffic. The unfinished arguments kept your seat warm. The people you love are exactly as difficult and as luminous as filed.`,
  "ending.return.beat3": `But you catch yourself, in the following weeks, doing small strange things: standing a moment longer in doorways. Reading faces like letters. Treating your own memories as a place you have visited rather than a country you rule.`,
  "ending.return.beat4": `You will lose almost all of it — the rooms, the fog, the Usher’s terrible jokes. It goes the way dreams go. What stays is subtler: a kind of weather. Whoever dissolved on the way in is not the one who walked out — and the strangest part, the part you never manage to say out loud, is that you would not trade the dissolving away.`,
  "ending.return.beat5": `The way back to reality, it turns out, was never a road. It was a renovation. You live there now.`,
  "ending.return.epitaph": `The world was exactly the same. That was never the promise.`,
  "ending.return.note.body": `Every wisdom tradition has a name for it: the descent that returns. Innana into the underworld, Jonah into the fish, the dark night of the soul, the hero into the labyrinth — the pattern is not the going down but the coming back changed, carrying something the surface could not have produced. Psychology calls the last step integration: an ego death matters only as much as the life rebuilt after it. The danger was never the dissolving. It was refusing to return — mistaking the depths for home — or returning and refusing to be different, sealing over. To come back ordinary, but rearranged: that was always the whole assignment.`,
  "ending.return.note.thinkers": `the night-sea journey · integration`,
  "ending.return.note.title": `On Coming Back`,
  "ending.return.title": `The Return`,
  "room.beggars-math.choice.donate-far.hint": `The larger number.`,
  "room.beggars-math.choice.donate-far.outcome0": `You do it before the rationalization can curdle into forgetting: double the lunch, confirmed, receipt in your inbox before the next corner.`,
  "room.beggars-math.choice.donate-far.outcome1": `The man watches you go. You will carry his face for a while, and the strange truth is that carrying it is part of the price — you paid in guilt for lives you will never see saved.`,
  "room.beggars-math.choice.donate-far.outcome2": `USHER: Cold, consistent, and correct by at least one respectable school of thought. The man in the shelter does not subscribe to its journal, of course.`,
  "room.beggars-math.choice.donate-far.text": `Nod an apology, walk on, and donate double to the nets.`,
  "room.beggars-math.choice.give-him.hint": `The person in front of you.`,
  "room.beggars-math.choice.give-him.outcome0": `You come back with soup and coffee and sit on your heels in the rain while he eats. He tells you his name. The name is the transaction the money only carried.`,
  "room.beggars-math.choice.give-him.outcome1": `Somewhere far away, the arithmetic continues without you. It always was going to. Arithmetic is patient; the man in the shelter was not going to be here for the argument.`,
  "room.beggars-math.choice.give-him.outcome2": `You chose the face. The face is not a fallacy. It might be a foundation.`,
  "room.beggars-math.choice.give-him.text": `Kneel. Give him the money, and get him something hot.`,
  "room.beggars-math.choice.grand-gesture.hint": `Multiply the good. Be seen doing it.`,
  "room.beggars-math.choice.grand-gesture.outcome0": `The video does well. The framing is respectful, mostly. The man looks into your lens the way people look into weather.`,
  "room.beggars-math.choice.grand-gesture.outcome1": `Notice what the camera changed: the gift now has two recipients, and one of them is your reflection. “Awareness” was raised. So was something else, quietly, in the algorithm and in you.`,
  "room.beggars-math.choice.grand-gesture.outcome2": `USHER: (as a comment, first, pinned) Bravo! The best part is you can’t tell anymore which part was for him. Neither can I, and I’m professionally omniscient on Tuesdays.`,
  "room.beggars-math.choice.grand-gesture.text": `Give big, publicly — film it, tag the charity, raise awareness.`,
  "room.beggars-math.choice.split.hint": `Both hands.`,
  "room.beggars-math.choice.split.outcome0": `Half a lunch and half a net. The compromise feels wise for exactly one block, and then feels like what it is: two half-answers holding hands.`,
  "room.beggars-math.choice.split.outcome1": `And yet — he ate something, and somewhere a child sleeps under half your arithmetic. Perhaps the split was not for them at all. Perhaps it was so you could keep both of your selves on speaking terms.`,
  "room.beggars-math.choice.split.text": `Split it — half to him now, half to the nets tonight.`,
  "room.beggars-math.doorHint": `The door of the near and the far`,
  "room.beggars-math.note.body": `Peter Singer’s famous argument begins with a child drowning in a shallow pond: you would ruin your shoes to save her without hesitation, so why does distance change the duty? If suffering is bad and you can prevent it at trivial cost, geography is morally arbitrary — the far child and the near one weigh the same. Effective altruism builds on this: measure, compare, send the money where it verifiably does most. The critics answer that morality grown entirely from arithmetic loses something load-bearing — that the face in front of you is not a bias to be corrected but the primal scene where obligation is learned at all; erode it and the far child eventually loses her claim too. Note the trap this room set: the choice that felt most generous, the public gesture, scored worst — because its true beneficiary had crept behind the camera. Both honest options were defensible. Only the performance was not.`,
  "room.beggars-math.note.thinkers": `Peter Singer · effective altruism and its critics`,
  "room.beggars-math.note.title": `The Drowning Child`,
  "room.beggars-math.stage0.beat0": `A bus shelter in freezing rain. A man is folded into the corner of it, wet past the point where wet means anything, and he asks you — politely, precisely — for the price of a lunch.`,
  "room.beggars-math.stage0.beat1": `Your phone is in your hand. On the screen, by coincidence or by curriculum, is a charity page you were reading: verified, audited, cost-effective. The same lunch money, it says, buys anti-malarial nets. Statistically, over time, the arithmetic saves lives — actual, countable lives, far away.`,
  "room.beggars-math.stage0.beat2": `The man is three feet from you. The lives are three thousand miles away. The money is the same money.`,
  "room.beggars-math.stage0.beat3": `USHER: (as a voice on the charity page’s chat widget) Hello! I’m the Helper. Both buttons are real. That’s the whole problem. How can I assist?`,
  "room.beggars-math.title": `The Beggar’s Math`,
  "room.boulder.choice.carry-well.hint": `The stone is the road.`,
  "room.boulder.choice.carry-well.outcome0": `You stack the stones. You do it neither fast nor slow, neither expecting success nor performing defeat — you do it the way one does a thing that is simply one’s thing to do. And something shifts, not in the hill but in the grammar: the task stops being an obstacle between you and the dawn, and becomes the place where you happen to be alive right now.`,
  "room.boulder.choice.carry-well.outcome1": `The stones come down. You notice you are not waiting for them not to. You begin again, and the beginning-again is not failure repeating — it is a rhythm, and rhythms belong to the drummer.`,
  "room.boulder.choice.carry-well.outcome2": `The man stands, dusts off his hands, and does something the Usher later swears has never happened in eleven millennia: he helps. Two stackers, one impossible task, dawn coming up like a held note. “You see it,” he says. It is not a question. For one full stack — before it falls, and it does fall — the hill is, unmistakably, happy.`,
  "room.boulder.choice.carry-well.text": `Begin again — without hope and without despair. Make the carrying yours.`,
  "room.boulder.choice.irony.hint": `If it’s absurd, be absurd back.`,
  "room.boulder.choice.irony.outcome0": `You begin narrating the task like a nature documentary. You stack the stones into insulting shapes. You give each stone a name and a backstory and fire one of them for insubordination.`,
  "room.boulder.choice.irony.outcome1": `The man laughs — a sound like something unrusting — and the Usher applauds from the thermos. The stones still come down. But the hill, you notice, has stopped being heavy in one specific way: it cannot humiliate someone who refuses to hold it sacred. Irony is not the deepest answer, the man tells you, wiping his eyes. “But it got me through the fourth century, and I won’t hear a word against it.”`,
  "room.boulder.choice.irony.text": `Laugh at it. Stack the stones badly, on purpose, with commentary.`,
  "room.boulder.choice.rage.hint": `Fight the stone.`,
  "room.boulder.choice.rage.outcome0": `You attack the task. You develop techniques, footholds, a genuinely impressive throwing method. The stones come down. You go again. The stones come down. There is a purity in the fury, for a while — the hill becomes the enemy, and an enemy is at least a companion.`,
  "room.boulder.choice.rage.outcome1": `You stop, eventually, lungs burning, and notice the man watching you with deep recognition. “Year one,” he says softly. “Good year, actually.” The rage was real and the rage was fuel and the rage changed nothing but you — which, a small voice notes, is the only thing anything changes here.`,
  "room.boulder.choice.rage.text": `Rage at it. Stack faster, harder — make the hill regret this.`,
  "room.boulder.choice.refuse-carry.hint": `The strike. It will cost you.`,
  "room.boulder.choice.refuse-carry.outcome0": `You sit down among the stones and fold the whole task away from you. It is not laziness — it is a position: some assignments deserve refusal, and mandatory futility is surely one.`,
  "room.boulder.choice.refuse-carry.outcome1": `The room does not punish you with thunder. It does something quieter: nothing. The dawn stays exactly where it is, unearned, and the hours pass through you, and you feel — precisely, physically — a heart’s worth of grip loosen. Not because refusal is wrong. Because refusal, here, is a vote to stop moving, and stillness in this place has a current, and the current pulls one way.`,
  "room.boulder.choice.refuse-carry.outcome2": `USHER: (draping a coat over your shoulders) The strike is noted, and honestly, respected. But the hill is the only road, friend. Even he — (nodding at the man) — pushes. Not because it means something. Because he’s decided the pushing is his. Come on. Up.`,
  "room.boulder.choice.refuse-carry.text": `Refuse. Sit down. You will not perform meaninglessness for anyone.`,
  "room.boulder.doorHint": `The door at the bottom of the hill`,
  "room.boulder.note.body": `Camus called it the only serious philosophical problem: if life has no given meaning, why continue it? His answer begins with Sisyphus, condemned by the gods to roll a boulder uphill forever, watching it fall each time. Camus refuses both exits — suicide (yielding to meaninglessness) and what he called philosophical suicide (leaping into consoling systems that promise the meaning back). The absurd, for him, is not the world’s property; it is the collision between our demand for meaning and the universe’s silence — and it must be kept alive, not resolved. The scandalous last line — “one must imagine Sisyphus happy” — is not optimism. It is the claim that in the walk back down, lucid, unconsoled, and continuing anyway, Sisyphus becomes superior to his punishment: the boulder is his boulder, the rhythm his own. This room could not be won because the point was never the stack. It was the walk down: the moment the task stopped happening to you and began, precisely, to be yours.`,
  "room.boulder.note.thinkers": `Albert Camus · The Myth of Sisyphus · the absurd`,
  "room.boulder.note.title": `Sisyphus, Happy`,
  "room.boulder.stage0.beat0": `The fog is thinning. Through it, for the first time, something like dawn — a horizon with an actual color. And between you and the horizon: a hill, and a task.`,
  "room.boulder.stage0.beat1": `Stones. A neat sign: STACK THE STONES TO PROCEED. You stack them. On the placement of the final stone, the stack unstacks itself — not violently, almost apologetically, the stones rolling back to their exact starting positions like employees returning from a break.`,
  "room.boulder.stage0.beat2": `You try again. Same result. The room, to its credit, is honest: a second sign, smaller, lower down, reads: THE TASK CANNOT BE COMPLETED. THE TASK IS ALSO MANDATORY. WE ARE AWARE OF THE TENSION.`,
  "room.boulder.stage0.beat3": `A man is sitting on one of the stones. He looks like he has been here since the Bronze Age, because he has. He nods at you — not unkindly — and gestures at the hill with the weariness of a colleague.`,
  "room.boulder.stage0.beat4": `USHER: (arriving with a thermos) Ah. You’ve met our longest-serving resident. He’s tried everything: engineering, prayer, litigation. The stones always come down. The only variable anyone has ever moved — (pouring two cups, one for the man) — is the carrying.`,
  "room.boulder.title": `The Boulder`,
  "room.casino-pascal.choice.bet-devil.hint": `Empiricism, of a kind.`,
  "room.casino-pascal.choice.bet-devil.outcome0": `The Usher’s grin achieves structural significance.`,
  "room.casino-pascal.choice.bet-devil.outcome1": `USHER: A bet on the visible! I admire the epistemology and question the judgment. Yes, I’m demonstrably here — but think it through: if I exist, the other position gains considerable inferential support. You’ve bet on the doorman as evidence against the building.`,
  "room.casino-pascal.choice.bet-devil.outcome2": `USHER: Still. First traveler in an eon to bet on present company. (slides one chip back) Loyalty rate. Don’t spend it on anything eternal.`,
  "room.casino-pascal.choice.bet-devil.text": `Bet on the Devil. At least the Devil is definitely at the table.`,
  "room.casino-pascal.choice.bet-god.hint": `The expected value is literally infinite.`,
  "room.casino-pascal.choice.bet-god.outcome0": `You slide the chips across. The Usher makes them vanish with the smoothness of long practice.`,
  "room.casino-pascal.choice.bet-god.outcome1": `USHER: Splendid. Now — small print. Which God did you just bet on? I ask because the vault has many windows, and a wager placed to hedge is visible from all of them. If He’s the sort who reads motives, you’ve just handed Him an actuarial table with your name on it. Faith as portfolio strategy. He may pay out. He may laugh. Historically, laughing is free.`,
  "room.casino-pascal.choice.bet-god.outcome2": `USHER: (pocketing one chip) Handling fee. For the other guy. Which, tonight, is also me. Efficient, no?`,
  "room.casino-pascal.choice.bet-god.text": `Bet on God. Infinity times anything beats everything.`,
  "room.casino-pascal.choice.interrogate.hint": `Audit the house before playing it.`,
  "room.casino-pascal.choice.interrogate.outcome0": `The Usher stops shuffling. In the sudden quiet you can hear the casino recalculating you.`,
  "room.casino-pascal.choice.interrogate.outcome1": `USHER: (slowly, with real pleasure) Ohh. There it is. The only question the table can’t cover. The wager pretends there are two outcomes — my God, or nothing. But seat a thousand gods, each with an infinite payout and mutually exclusive terms, and the mathematics jams: infinity divided by everything, times whatever the dealer isn’t telling you. The bet was never about God. It was about who got to design the menu.`,
  "room.casino-pascal.choice.interrogate.outcome2": `USHER: House rule seventeen: anyone who audits the house drinks free. (produces something that steams) You’ve earned this. Don’t ask what’s in it. That’s question two.`,
  "room.casino-pascal.choice.interrogate.text": `“Who sets these odds? And which gods are on the menu?”`,
  "room.casino-pascal.choice.refuse-bet.hint": `Doxastic honesty.`,
  "room.casino-pascal.choice.refuse-bet.outcome0": `You keep your chips. The Usher nods, unoffended — the house profits from refusals too, in ways the house declines to explain.`,
  "room.casino-pascal.choice.refuse-bet.outcome1": `USHER: Fair. You can’t make yourself believe for money any more than you can fall in love for tax purposes. Pascal knew that, by the way — his actual advice was subtler: act as if, keep the pews warm, and let belief grow in like a habit. Whether that’s wisdom or self-hypnosis with incense is question two. Nobody stays for question two. They’re always off to question one at some other table.`,
  "room.casino-pascal.choice.refuse-bet.text": `Refuse to bet. Belief isn’t a thing a wallet can do.`,
  "room.casino-pascal.doorHint": `The door with the infinite jackpot`,
  "room.casino-pascal.note.body": `Pascal, inventor of probability theory, proposed treating God as a decision problem: if you believe and He exists, you gain infinity; if He doesn’t, you lose little. Expected value says bet. The wager’s brilliance is that it sidesteps evidence entirely — and that is also its wound. The many-gods objection notes that the decision matrix is missing rows: bet on the wrong deity and the payout may invert; with a thousand candidate gods, infinite payouts collide and the arithmetic seizes. Deeper still: belief may not be wagerable — you cannot believe at will, only act as if, and a God worth the name presumably audits motives. And infinities break decision theory itself: any nonzero chance of infinite reward swallows every finite concern, licensing fanaticism of all flavors. The winning move in this casino was never a bet. It was noticing who built the table, wrote the menu, and smiled while calling it your free choice.`,
  "room.casino-pascal.note.thinkers": `Blaise Pascal · the many-gods objection · decision under uncertainty`,
  "room.casino-pascal.note.title": `Pascal’s Wager`,
  "room.casino-pascal.stage0.beat0": `A casino has been installed in the machinery, all brass and green felt, lit like a sermon. Every game is unattended except one.`,
  "room.casino-pascal.stage0.beat1": `At the center table stands the Usher — horns polished, halo in a coat-check somewhere — dealing cards to nobody with tremendous professionalism.`,
  "room.casino-pascal.stage0.beat2": `USHER: (Devil mode, unmistakably enjoying it) One table, one wager, house classic. Bet that God exists. If He does: infinite payout — eternity, bliss, the works. If He doesn’t: you lose nothing. A finite stake against an infinite return. Mathematically, friend, you cannot afford NOT to bet. House rules.`,
  "room.casino-pascal.stage0.beat3": `The chips are small and warm, and you realize each one is stamped with a year of your life. You appear to have a pocketful.`,
  "room.casino-pascal.title": `The Casino of Pascal`,
  "room.court-of-usher.choice.command-makes-good.hint": `Divine command, straight up.`,
  "room.court-of-usher.choice.command-makes-good.outcome0": `USHER: (prosecution, delighted; defense, horrified) Then hear the sentence that follows: if I commanded cruelty tomorrow — the torture of innocents, on letterhead — it would thereby be GOOD. Same signature, same authority. “Good” would mean nothing but “ordered.”`,
  "room.court-of-usher.choice.command-makes-good.outcome1": `USHER: You’ve made me omnipotent and made goodness arbitrary in a single ruling. Half of me is thrilled. The other half wants you to know that the half that’s thrilled is exactly the half you should be worried about.`,
  "room.court-of-usher.choice.command-makes-good.text": `“Good because you command it. Authority is the ground floor.”`,
  "room.court-of-usher.choice.good-precedes.hint": `The standard stands above the throne.`,
  "room.court-of-usher.choice.good-precedes.outcome0": `USHER: (defense, relieved; prosecution, filing an appeal) So goodness exists independently, and I merely — enforce it. Announce it. Do the paperwork.`,
  "room.court-of-usher.choice.good-precedes.outcome1": `USHER: (both roles at once, staring at you) Then I’m middle management. The good is my employer, and worship is a category error — you should be worshipping the standard and sending ME a card at holidays. Do you know how long I’ve suspected this? Eleven millennia. It’s why I drink on even days. Which, before you ask, is also both days.`,
  "room.court-of-usher.choice.good-precedes.text": `“You command it because it’s good. Goodness outranks you.”`,
  "room.court-of-usher.choice.spot-horns.hint": `Refuse the dilemma’s framing — show why.`,
  "room.court-of-usher.choice.spot-horns.outcome0": `The courtroom stops. Even the machinery leans in.`,
  "room.court-of-usher.choice.spot-horns.outcome1": `You lay it out: horn one makes goodness arbitrary — mere decree. Horn two makes the divine redundant — a herald for a standard it didn’t author. But both horns assume “good” is a finished thing waiting to be located, above the throne or below it. Define what goodness IS — flourishing, love’s structure, the shape of a life that works — and the dilemma stops goring and starts describing: perhaps the divine and the good aren’t ranked, but identical, or entangled past separating.`,
  "room.court-of-usher.choice.spot-horns.outcome2": `USHER: (removing, slowly, both the horns and the halo, holding them in the same hand) Twenty-two thousand travelers. Four have done that. The other three are... well, you may meet one, depending on your route. (leaning close, quieter than the room) I’ll remember this. This place forgets everything eventually — every plank, every photograph. But I’ll remember this.`,
  "room.court-of-usher.choice.spot-horns.text": `“The question is the trap. Define ‘good’ first, or both horns gore you.”`,
  "room.court-of-usher.choice.you-dont-exist.hint": `The atheist gambit, in court.`,
  "room.court-of-usher.choice.you-dont-exist.outcome0": `USHER: (checking under the bench, patting himself down) Hm. The evidence is against you locally, but I take the point globally.`,
  "room.court-of-usher.choice.you-dont-exist.outcome1": `USHER: For what it’s worth, that IS a position with standing — it just relocates the problem instead of solving it. Strike the commander from the dilemma and the hard question remains, wearing different robes: is anything good, and says who? You’ve dismissed the defendant. The charge is still at large. (stamping a form) Motion noted. HR takes your position too, incidentally. It’s why our meetings are so short.`,
  "room.court-of-usher.choice.you-dont-exist.text": `“Objection: the defendant doesn’t exist. Case dismissed.”`,
  "room.court-of-usher.doorHint": `The door with the summons nailed to it`,
  "room.court-of-usher.note.body": `In Plato’s Euthyphro, Socrates corners a confident young man on the courthouse steps: is the pious loved by the gods because it is pious, or pious because they love it? Twenty-four centuries later the horns are still sharp. Take the first horn — good because commanded — and morality becomes arbitrary decree: had the commands been cruel, cruelty would be good, and “God is good” collapses into “God is God.” Take the second — commanded because good — and the standard of goodness stands above the divine, making the commander a messenger and the worship misdirected. Divine command theorists have spent lifetimes between the horns; the most durable escape, from Aquinas to modern natural-law thought, is to deny the premise that goodness and the divine nature are two things that could be ranked at all — the good is not decreed nor obeyed but constitutive, the grain of reality itself. Whether that dissolves the dilemma or merely renames it is, as the Usher would say, question two.`,
  "room.court-of-usher.note.thinkers": `Plato · divine command theory · natural law`,
  "room.court-of-usher.note.title": `The Euthyphro Dilemma`,
  "room.court-of-usher.stage0.beat0": `A courtroom assembled from the machinery — the judge’s bench is a decommissioned altar, the witness stand a confession booth turned inside out.`,
  "room.court-of-usher.stage0.beat1": `The Usher occupies every position at once, with visible strain: prosecution (horns forward), defense (halo tilted sympathetically), and defendant (both, flickering).`,
  "room.court-of-usher.stage0.beat2": `USHER: (banging a gavel, then objecting to it) The case is existential and the docket is backed up eleven millennia, so we’ll be brief. You are the judge. Yes, you. The dissolved one. You’re the only party here without a conflict of interest, which tells you everything about this jurisdiction.`,
  "room.court-of-usher.stage0.beat3": `USHER: (as defendant, suddenly quiet and serious) Here is the charge I bring against myself. When I say a thing is good — is it good because I command it? Or do I command it because it is good? Choose carefully. My entire employment structure depends on your answer. Possibly also the concept of employment. Possibly the concept of concepts.`,
  "room.court-of-usher.title": `The Court of the Usher`,
  "room.debt-of-dead.choice.ask-question.hint": `The question you never got to ask.`,
  "room.debt-of-dead.choice.ask-question.outcome0": `You ask the question that has been standing in every doorway of your life, and the dying, who have no remaining use for their defenses, answer it.`,
  "room.debt-of-dead.choice.ask-question.outcome1": `The answer is smaller than the wound. It always is. Not malice on a throne — a frightened person, a weak moment metastasized, reasons that would embarrass a schoolchild. You expected an architecture of cruelty and were handed a shrug that broke your life. The disappointment is its own strange medicine: nothing that small deserves the throne it has occupied in you.`,
  "room.debt-of-dead.choice.ask-question.outcome2": `What you say after — yes, or no, or rest now — matters less than you thought it would. The question was the visit. They answer it, and you both know the account was audited at last, by the only two people who ever held it.`,
  "room.debt-of-dead.choice.ask-question.text": `“First — why? Tell me the truth of it. Then I’ll answer.”`,
  "room.debt-of-dead.choice.forgive-silently.hint": `Release the debt without handing them the receipt.`,
  "room.debt-of-dead.choice.forgive-silently.outcome0": `Inside, you open the hand. The stone goes. You feel it go — it is real, the release, as real as any spoken version.`,
  "room.debt-of-dead.choice.forgive-silently.outcome1": `Out loud you say only, “Rest now,” and their eyes search your face for the verdict, and find kindness where the verdict would be, which they will spend their last hours reading in both directions.`,
  "room.debt-of-dead.choice.forgive-silently.outcome2": `You kept the forgiveness and withheld the absolution — freed yourself without unlocking their door. Notice, without judgment, that this was the one option where the mercy stayed entirely on your side of the bed. Perhaps that was wisdom. Perhaps it was the last small installment of the debt, paid in their currency after all.`,
  "room.debt-of-dead.choice.forgive-silently.text": `Forgive them — in your chest, fully — and say only: “Rest now.”`,
  "room.debt-of-dead.choice.forgive.hint": `Release the debt — for whoever it frees.`,
  "room.debt-of-dead.choice.forgive.outcome0": `You say it. The words cost more than you expected and weigh less once they are out — as if you had been holding a stone against a current and finally opened your hand, and discovered the current had been holding you too.`,
  "room.debt-of-dead.choice.forgive.outcome1": `Their face does what faces do when a life’s last account unexpectedly clears. They do not say thank you. They say your name — correctly, gently — and something in the ledger between you, which was never going to balance, simply closes instead.`,
  "room.debt-of-dead.choice.forgive.outcome2": `Forgiving did not make what they did acceptable. It made it finished. You will spend years learning that those are different words, and tonight was the first lesson.`,
  "room.debt-of-dead.choice.forgive.text": `“Yes. I forgive you.” Say it, and mean it as far as you can.`,
  "room.debt-of-dead.choice.refuse.hint": `Honesty over absolution.`,
  "room.debt-of-dead.choice.refuse.outcome0": `You say no. You say it without spite — that is the part that costs — and you stay in the chair, which says the thing the no cannot: that they mattered enough for the wound to be real, and the wound is real enough that its owner will not sign it away at a deathbed’s convenience.`,
  "room.debt-of-dead.choice.refuse.outcome1": `They receive it. Somewhere under the machines’ courtesy, you see something almost like respect: they asked an honest question at the end of their life and got an honest answer, which is more than most endings are furnished with.`,
  "room.debt-of-dead.choice.refuse.outcome2": `You held the line, and stayed anyway. Some debts are recorded honestly rather than settled falsely, and the record, too, is a kind of tribute — proof that what happened happened, to someone who was really there.`,
  "room.debt-of-dead.choice.refuse.text": `“No. I’m here — that’s what I have. Forgiveness isn’t.”`,
  "room.debt-of-dead.doorHint": `The door you swore you’d never knock on`,
  "room.debt-of-dead.note.body": `Bishop Butler defined forgiveness as the forswearing of resentment — not excusing, not forgetting, not reconciling, but declining to keep paying interest on the wound. Hannah Arendt called forgiveness the only genuine escape from the past’s irreversibility: without it, we remain forever the victim of one deed, and the wrongdoer forever its author; forgiveness releases both parties from a frozen frame. But Derrida saw the paradox at the bedside: forgiveness worth the name forgives the unforgivable — anything less is mere accounting, waiving debts already affordable. And the deathbed sharpens every edge: a sincere apology changes the wrongdoer, not the wrong; your forgiveness is asked for at the exact moment refusing it becomes cruel and granting it can feel coerced. There was no correct answer in that chair. Forgiving, refusing honestly, releasing silently, and asking the unasked question are four different acts of taking the wound seriously — which was, perhaps, the only debt that was truly yours to pay.`,
  "room.debt-of-dead.note.thinkers": `Bishop Butler · Hannah Arendt · Jacques Derrida`,
  "room.debt-of-dead.note.title": `Forgiveness and the Ledger`,
  "room.debt-of-dead.stage0.beat0": `A bedside. The room has arranged it with terrible specificity — the smell of a hospital trying to smell like lavender, a window with evening in it, a chair placed for you as if you had confirmed attendance.`,
  "room.debt-of-dead.stage0.beat1": `In the bed is the one who wronged you. The room has left the face slightly blurred, because it is not one person — it is whoever your chest just named. The wrong was real. This place does not deal in misunderstandings; what they did, they did, and it shaped the floor plan of your life.`,
  "room.debt-of-dead.stage0.beat2": `They are dying. Actually, imminently — the machines have moved from urgency to courtesy. And they are awake, and they know you, and they ask, in a voice with almost nothing left in it: “Can you forgive me?”`,
  "room.debt-of-dead.stage0.beat3": `They mean it. That is the room’s one cruelty: you can see that they mean it, and that meaning it changes nothing about what was done. Both facts, fully lit, at the same bedside.`,
  "room.debt-of-dead.title": `The Debt of the Dead`,
  "room.dinner-table.choice.deflect.hint": `True, and not an answer.`,
  "room.dinner-table.choice.deflect.outcome0": `A sentence engineered to be true and to say nothing — the diplomat of sentences. She hears both halves.`,
  "room.dinner-table.choice.deflect.outcome1": `“Yes,” she says, and looks out the window, releasing you from the question the way you release a bird that turned out to be too small to eat.`,
  "room.dinner-table.choice.deflect.outcome2": `The tea gets colder. Something honest almost happened here, and you both agreed to miss it politely.`,
  "room.dinner-table.choice.deflect.text": `“The doctors are doing everything they can.”`,
  "room.dinner-table.choice.kind-lie.hint": `The mercy of it.`,
  "room.dinner-table.choice.kind-lie.outcome0": `Her shoulders come down. She talks about spring — the garden, specifically, the argument she intends to win against the slugs.`,
  "room.dinner-table.choice.kind-lie.outcome1": `The evening is warm and the warmth is real even if its foundation is not. You bought it. She spent it well.`,
  "room.dinner-table.choice.kind-lie.outcome2": `You will never know if she believed you, or let you believe she believed you, so that you would be all right.`,
  "room.dinner-table.choice.kind-lie.text": `“Of course you are. By spring.”`,
  "room.dinner-table.choice.silence.hint": `No words at all.`,
  "room.dinner-table.choice.silence.outcome0": `You do not answer. You put your other hand over hers, and you stay, and the clock does whatever clocks do in rooms like this.`,
  "room.dinner-table.choice.silence.outcome1": `She studies you. She has known you since before you could lie, and she reads the answer in the silence — but a silence can be read gently, at whatever speed the reader can bear.`,
  "room.dinner-table.choice.silence.outcome2": `“You always were the quiet one,” she says, which was never true, which means: I understand. Thank you for not making me hear it.`,
  "room.dinner-table.choice.silence.text": `Say nothing. Hold her hand and stay.`,
  "room.dinner-table.choice.truth.hint": `What she asked for, maybe.`,
  "room.dinner-table.choice.truth.outcome0": `You say it. The words are worse in the air than they were in your chest, and then, strangely, better.`,
  "room.dinner-table.choice.truth.outcome1": `She is quiet for a long time. Then: “Good. I hate being handled.” She squeezes your hand once, like signing something.`,
  "room.dinner-table.choice.truth.outcome2": `You will never know if that was relief on her face or the last kindness she did you — pretending your honesty was a gift.`,
  "room.dinner-table.choice.truth.text": `Tell her the truth, gently and whole.`,
  "room.dinner-table.doorHint": `The door of the question you already know`,
  "room.dinner-table.note.body": `Kant, notoriously, held that lying is wrong even to a murderer asking where your friend is hiding: a lie treats its hearer as a mechanism to be operated rather than a rational being owed the truth, and universalized, it corrodes the very possibility of trust that language runs on. The ethics of care replies that morality does not live in universals — it lives in this room, this hand, this particular person and what they can carry tonight. A dying woman’s question may not be a request for information at all; it may be a request for accompaniment, and answering the literal question could be the real betrayal. There is no configuration of words that is safe here. The truth steals hope; the lie steals her chance to say goodbye knowingly; the deflection steals the moment itself. You always pass this room, because passing was never the point. The lesson is the weight.`,
  "room.dinner-table.note.thinkers": `Kant · Benjamin Constant · Carol Gilligan, ethics of care`,
  "room.dinner-table.note.title": `The Kind Lie`,
  "room.dinner-table.stage0.beat0": `A kitchen you almost recognize. Tea going cold in the good cups, the ones only used when something is wrong.`,
  "room.dinner-table.stage0.beat1": `Your grandmother sits across from you. She has been elsewhere for weeks — the long, dissolving elsewhere — but tonight her eyes are her own, sudden and clear, and she has been waiting for you specifically.`,
  "room.dinner-table.stage0.beat2": `She takes your hand. Her grip is the strongest thing left in her.`,
  "room.dinner-table.stage0.beat3": `“I’m going to get better,” she says. “Aren’t I.”`,
  "room.dinner-table.stage0.beat4": `It is barely a question. It is a door she is holding open, and you cannot tell from which side.`,
  "room.dinner-table.title": `The Dinner Table`,
  "room.door-that-asks.choice.changed-mind.hint": `Growth, named and owned.`,
  "room.door-that-asks.choice.changed-mind.outcome0": `You name the room. The specific one. And you name what changed — not a mood, a reason: something a later room taught the earlier one.`,
  "room.door-that-asks.choice.changed-mind.outcome1": `THE DOOR: (a sound like a lock deciding to be a hinge) That is the answer I am for. Consistency is respectable; revision with receipts is rarer and better. A self that cannot change its mind is not steady — it is finished, and finished is the one thing you have spent this whole journey declining to be.`,
  "room.door-that-asks.choice.changed-mind.outcome2": `THE DOOR: For the record: the traveler who leaves is not the one who arrived, and you are the rare kind who knows it in writing.`,
  "room.door-that-asks.choice.changed-mind.text": `“No — not all of it. Some answers I’d change, and I can tell you exactly what changed me.”`,
  "room.door-that-asks.choice.dont-remember.hint": `The honest gap.`,
  "room.door-that-asks.choice.dont-remember.outcome1": `THE DOOR: Very well. The interview concludes. What remains is not a question but a threshold.`,
  "room.door-that-asks.choice.dont-remember.text": `“Some of it I don’t even remember choosing. I was dissolving at the time.”`,
  "room.door-that-asks.choice.laughing-door.hint": `You earned the noticing.`,
  "room.door-that-asks.choice.laughing-door.outcome0": `You cross to the small plain door, and the handle turns before you have quite gripped it, the way a friend opens from the other side.`,
  "room.door-that-asks.choice.laughing-door.outcome1": `USHER: (behind you, and for once his voice has neither halo nor horns in it) Twenty-two thousand travelers. Four noticed the courtroom trap. Fewer opened this. Go on, then. I’ll get the lights.`,
  "room.door-that-asks.choice.laughing-door.text": `The small door. The laughter. Open it.`,
  "room.door-that-asks.choice.lie-down.hint": `The quiet.`,
  "room.door-that-asks.choice.lie-down.outcome0": `You lie down at the threshold with the morning three steps away, and it is not defeat — the room can tell, the Usher can tell, even the fog can tell. It is a choice, made with open eyes, by someone who walked every room to earn the right to make it.`,
  "room.door-that-asks.choice.lie-down.outcome1": `USHER: (sitting down beside you, taking off both hats for good) Then I’ll stay till it’s done. Nobody dissolves alone on my shift. (a pause) It’s not an ending, you know. It’s a tide. Everything the rooms filed goes back to the sea, and the sea — (his voice already sounding like water) — has never once lost a single thing that mattered.`,
  "room.door-that-asks.choice.lie-down.text": `Lie down at the threshold. Let the dissolving finish, gently, on your own terms.`,
  "room.door-that-asks.choice.stand-by-it.hint": `Consistency, owned.`,
  "room.door-that-asks.choice.stand-by-it.outcome0": `THE DOOR: Steadiness. It is rarer than it advertises. Most travelers disown at least one room the moment a door asks — you kept the whole ledger, including the entries that cost you.`,
  "room.door-that-asks.choice.stand-by-it.outcome1": `THE DOOR: I note, without cruelty, that a ledger fully signed can be integrity or armor; from this side of the grille they are identical. You will know which it was later, at some unscheduled hour. They always find out at unscheduled hours.`,
  "room.door-that-asks.choice.stand-by-it.text": `“Yes. All of it. I chose what I chose, and I’d sign it again.”`,
  "room.door-that-asks.choice.stay.hint": `The keeper’s bargain.`,
  "room.door-that-asks.choice.stay.outcome0": `You turn from the morning — actually turn from it, which the threshold registers with something like a bow — and hold out your hand for the clipboard.`,
  "room.door-that-asks.choice.stay.outcome1": `USHER: (not taking it out yet) Be sure. The hours are eternal, the pay is nothing, the travelers are — well, you’ve been one. You’ll watch every one of them face the fire and the lever and the folder, and you may never tell them the answers, chiefly because there aren’t any.`,
  "room.door-that-asks.choice.stay.outcome2": `USHER: (handing it over, and the clipboard is warm, and has your name on it — your real one, suddenly legible) ...Welcome aboard. First lesson: the halo and the horns are the same size. It’s on purpose. Everything here is.`,
  "room.door-that-asks.choice.stay.text": `Stay. Take the clipboard. Tend the rooms for whoever comes next.`,
  "room.door-that-asks.choice.walk-through.hint": `The return.`,
  "room.door-that-asks.choice.walk-through.outcome0": `You step toward the light. The threshold has the temperature of a doorway in summer — that half-degree change that means outside.`,
  "room.door-that-asks.choice.walk-through.outcome1": `USHER: (calling after you) Traveler. Whatever you find out there — it will be exactly the same world. That was never the promise. YOU were the renovation. Mind the gap.`,
  "room.door-that-asks.choice.walk-through.text": `Walk through. Back to the morning, the noise, the world.`,
  "room.door-that-asks.doorHint": `The last door, which is reading your file`,
  "room.door-that-asks.note.body": `Socrates said the unexamined life is not worth living, and was put to death by a jury that preferred not to be examined. This door is the sentence run in reverse: the examination, at last, of the examiner. Narrative identity theorists — MacIntyre, Ricoeur — hold that a self is not a substance but a story under continuous revision, and that the moral question is whether you can stand behind your story as both author and character. Note what the door rewarded: not consistency, which can calcify into armor, but revision with receipts — the ability to say “I changed my mind, and here is what changed it.” Kierkegaard: life is lived forward and understood backward; the door is where the two directions finally meet and compare notes. Every philosophy this journey staged — the trolley, the machine, the planks, the wager, the basement, the folder — was always one question wearing costumes: when your own file is read back to you, is the signature yours?`,
  "room.door-that-asks.note.thinkers": `Socrates · Kierkegaard · narrative identity — MacIntyre, Ricoeur`,
  "room.door-that-asks.note.title": `The Examined Life, Audited`,
  "room.door-that-asks.stage0.beat0": `One door remains. It is not large or ornate — it is exact, the way the small room was exact — and set into it at eye height is a brass grille, like an old elevator, or a confessional.`,
  "room.door-that-asks.stage0.beat1": `THE DOOR: Good morning. Before I open, I conduct a short interview. Not a test — an audit. You have spent this journey answering rooms. I ask about the answers.`,
  "room.door-that-asks.stage0.beat4": `THE DOOR: Take your time with the last one. It is the only question I actually need answered, and it is about all of them at once. Everything you chose in these rooms — do you stand by it?`,
  "room.door-that-asks.stage1.beat0": `The grille slides shut, and the door — the last door — swings open onto light that is not fog.`,
  "room.door-that-asks.stage1.beat1": `It is morning out there. An actual one: traffic somewhere, a kettle somewhere, the world at its enormous ordinary business. The way back, present tense, three steps away.`,
  "room.door-that-asks.stage1.beat2": `USHER: (beside you, suddenly, hat in hands — both hats, actually) End of the line. Or start, depending on the direction you read it. The threshold takes you back — to the noise, the faces, the unfinished arguments, all of it. Most take it. It’s a good door. I polish it myself.`,
  "room.door-that-asks.stage1.beat3": `USHER: But it is not the only option, and regulations require I say so. You may stay — the rooms always need a keeper, and between us, I’ve been due a sabbatical since the Bronze Age. Or you may lie down here at the threshold and let the last of the dissolving finish; some travelers, at the end, choose the quiet, and it is not my place to call that losing.`,
  "room.door-that-asks.title": `The Door That Asks`,
  "room.editor.choice.keep-it.hint": `The whole ship, rot and all.`,
  "room.editor.choice.keep-it.outcome0": `You close the folder and slide it back across the desk, and the room accepts it with something like respect. The weight settles back where it always sat. It is not lighter. That was never the offer.`,
  "room.editor.choice.keep-it.outcome1": `But something has changed, minutely: you have now chosen it. The memory used to be something that happened to you. As of tonight it is something you carry on purpose — same stone, different grip.`,
  "room.editor.choice.keep-it.outcome2": `USHER: For the record, that’s the difference between a scar and a wound. Same tissue. One of them is finished being written by someone else.`,
  "room.editor.choice.keep-it.text": `Close the folder. Keep it. It’s yours.`,
  "room.editor.choice.read-first.hint": `Face it, then choose.`,
  "room.editor.choice.read-first.outcome0": `You open the folder. Reading it is exactly as bad as you feared and slightly different than you remembered — the memory, it turns out, had been redacting itself for years, sanding the worst edge, sharpening a different one. The document is the uncut version. You read to the end. You did not know you could.`,
  "room.editor.choice.read-first.outcome1": `And having read it, the pen looks different. Smaller. The memory is still terrible, but it is now terrible and witnessed — by the one witness whose testimony you can never lose. You leave it in the folder, unstruck. Not because it doesn’t hurt. Because it’s true, and you were there, and someone should have been.`,
  "room.editor.choice.read-first.outcome2": `USHER: (quietly) That’s the rarest one, you know. Most people choose between carrying and cutting. Almost nobody thinks to finally READ the thing. The pen’s been here eleven millennia. The chair you just sat in — that’s the actual instrument.`,
  "room.editor.choice.read-first.text": `Open the folder. Read it — all of it, properly — before deciding.`,
  "room.editor.choice.strike-it.hint": `The clean amputation.`,
  "room.editor.choice.strike-it.outcome0": `The pen is light. The line is short. The relief is immediate and enormous and real — this room does not cheat, and the weight lifts like a hand taken off your chest after years.`,
  "room.editor.choice.strike-it.outcome1": `And then, over the following corridors, you notice the edges of the hole. A certain kind of stranger’s grief you used to recognize on sight — now just weather on a face. A patience you had, hard-won from somewhere, now unfunded. The wound is gone. So is what the wound was holding up.`,
  "room.editor.choice.strike-it.outcome2": `USHER: (gently, filing the folder’s empty jacket) No judgment. Genuinely. Some loads are load-bearing and some are just load, and not even I can always tell which from the outside. You made the call from inside. That was the only seat with a view.`,
  "room.editor.choice.strike-it.text": `Strike it out. No one is obligated to carry that.`,
  "room.editor.doorHint": `The door of the red pen`,
  "room.editor.note.body": `Nietzsche’s formula for greatness was amor fati — to love one’s fate, wanting nothing to be different, “not merely bear what is necessary, still less conceal it… but love it.” Not because suffering is good, but because the self that judges the suffering was built by it: strike the worst chapter and the reader changes too. Modern memory science gives the thought experiment teeth — reconsolidation research shows memories are rewritten each time they are recalled, and drugs that blunt a memory’s emotional charge already exist. The question is no longer science fiction: if you could sand down grief, should you? The case for the pen is real; nobody is morally obligated to bleed for character development. The case against is quieter: pain often turns out to be load-bearing — the source of a specific empathy, a specific patience, a specific refusal to let others suffer unwitnessed. Kierkegaard said life is lived forward but understood backward. The folder is the backward part. Editors, be careful with it.`,
  "room.editor.note.thinkers": `Nietzsche, amor fati · memory reconsolidation · Kierkegaard`,
  "room.editor.note.title": `The Wound and the Ledger`,
  "room.editor.stage0.beat0": `A study, lamp-lit, the kind of quiet that has furniture in it. On the desk: a single manila folder with your name on it — your real one, the one you currently cannot remember, which the folder tactfully keeps face-down.`,
  "room.editor.stage0.beat1": `Inside the folder is your worst memory. The room does not need to tell you which one. Your body has already told you: the shoulders first, then the stomach, then the weather of the face.`,
  "room.editor.stage0.beat2": `Beside the folder, a red pen. The instructions are engraved on the pen itself, small: ONE STRIKE REMOVES IT. THE EVENT WILL HAVE STILL HAPPENED. YOU WILL SIMPLY NEVER HAVE BEEN THERE.`,
  "room.editor.stage0.beat3": `USHER: (in a librarian’s cardigan, shelving other people’s folders) No trick this time. The pen works. No monkey’s paw, no ironic sting hidden in clause nine. The only cost is the one printed on the tin: you, minus that. Take all the time you need. The lamp is patient and so, unusually, am I.`,
  "room.editor.title": `The Editor`,
  "room.experience-machine.choice.enter.hint": `The guarantee.`,
  "room.experience-machine.choice.enter.outcome0": `The pod closes like a kind decision. And then — summers. A porch you own. Work that matters and finishes. Someone laughing at your third-best joke because they know the top two are coming.`,
  "room.experience-machine.choice.enter.outcome1": `It is, in every measurable respect, the best stretch of existence you have ever had. You surface from it only because this facility, unlike the retail model, has a return policy.`,
  "room.experience-machine.choice.enter.outcome2": `USHER: (helping you out) Lovely, wasn’t it. Do notice the tense you just used — “wasn’t.” Everything in there conjugates that way, eventually. Even so: you now know what perfect feels like. Some travelers find that clarifying. Others find it a splinter. We’ll see which you are.`,
  "room.experience-machine.choice.enter.text": `Get in. Happiness is happiness; the substrate is a technicality.`,
  "room.experience-machine.choice.refuse.hint": `Reality, with all faults, as-is.`,
  "room.experience-machine.choice.refuse.outcome0": `You step back. The pod does not sulk; perfection has excellent manners.`,
  "room.experience-machine.choice.refuse.outcome1": `USHER: Interesting. You chose a reality that is, at this very moment, a burning metaphysical bureaucracy — over guaranteed bliss. Either you believe contact with the real is worth more than any amount of feeling good, or you simply don’t trust products with an infinity symbol in the model number. Both are defensible. One is philosophy and one is consumer protection.`,
  "room.experience-machine.choice.refuse.text": `Refuse. You want to do things, not dream them.`,
  "room.experience-machine.choice.trial.hint": `Informed consent, surely.`,
  "room.experience-machine.choice.trial.outcome0": `Five minutes, you say. The pod, which has heard this before, says nothing.`,
  "room.experience-machine.choice.trial.outcome1": `Inside, five minutes lasts two years. Good years. When the lid opens you come out with the specific grief of a life that never happened — homesick for people who are still in there, being perfectly imaginary without you.`,
  "room.experience-machine.choice.trial.outcome2": `USHER: The trial is the whole product, friend. Nobody buys the machine. They buy the next five minutes, repeatedly, forever. You’re out, which puts you in rare company. Walk it off. The homesickness fades to a shimmer. The shimmer, I’m told, never quite does.`,
  "room.experience-machine.choice.trial.text": `“Five minutes. Just to know what I’d be refusing.”`,
  "room.experience-machine.doorHint": `The door behind which nothing is wrong`,
  "room.experience-machine.note.body": `Robert Nozick’s 1974 thought experiment was aimed at the heart of hedonism — the theory that pleasure is the only thing valuable in itself. If that were true, he argued, you should plug into a machine that delivers a lifetime of perfectly convincing bliss. Most people refuse, and the refusal is the data. Nozick drew out three reasons: we want to do things, not just have the experience of doing them; we want to be a certain kind of person, and a body floating in a tank is no kind at all; and we want contact with reality itself, unwilling to trade the true world for a nicer rendering. The machine has only grown less hypothetical since — every feed and game and scroll is a low-resolution pod, billed in five-minute trials. The question the plaque asks is not “would you plug in?” It is: by what percentage are you already in, and who is the you that would know?`,
  "room.experience-machine.note.thinkers": `Robert Nozick · hedonism · authenticity`,
  "room.experience-machine.note.title": `The Experience Machine`,
  "room.experience-machine.stage0.beat0": `A warm alcove in the cold machinery, upholstered in a light that remembers summer. In the center: a pod, open, shaped exactly like relief.`,
  "room.experience-machine.stage0.beat1": `A brass plaque, recently polished: EXPERIENCE MACHINE — MK. ∞. Below it, smaller: a perfect life, indistinguishable from real, guaranteed happy. Your brain will never know. That is the feature.`,
  "room.experience-machine.stage0.beat2": `USHER: (dusting the plaque with genuine tenderness) Best product we offer. The simulations are flawless — love, meaning, small delicious problems that always resolve. Nobody who enters ever complains afterward.`,
  "room.experience-machine.stage0.beat3": `USHER: Think carefully about why that sentence is the most frightening thing in this entire facility. I’ll wait. The pod will also wait. The pod is extremely good at waiting.`,
  "room.experience-machine.title": `The Experience Machine`,
  "room.free-will.choice.dont-press.hint": `You can’t predict what never happens.`,
  "room.free-will.choice.dont-press.outcome0": `You fold your arms. Minutes pass. The room is very good at minutes.`,
  "room.free-will.choice.dont-press.outcome1": `USHER: (turning the board around, where it has said, all along: “Will attempt not-pressing. Adorable. Est. duration: four minutes.”) Abstention is also a move, you see — the board covers the whole option space, including the pride of standing outside it. (the bell rings, apologetically) Four minutes almost exactly. You should be pleased; most people crack at two.`,
  "room.free-will.choice.dont-press.text": `Press nothing. Fold your arms. Refuse the whole game.`,
  "room.free-will.choice.press-knowing.hint": `Stop fighting the forecast. Own the weather.`,
  "room.free-will.choice.press-knowing.outcome0": `You press blue — the exact button the board named — not in surrender but with the deliberateness of a signature. The bell rings. It sounds, this once, less like scoring and more like agreement.`,
  "room.free-will.choice.press-knowing.outcome1": `USHER: (taking off the visor) And there it is. The exit was never beating the board. The exit is noticing that “it was determined” and “I chose it” might be two descriptions of one event — the forecast and the weather, arguing over who made it rain. Your choosing IS part of the machinery; the machinery runs THROUGH the choosing, not around it.`,
  "room.free-will.choice.press-knowing.outcome2": `USHER: (tearing the betting slip in half) I still won, technically. But you’re the first today to make winning feel beside the point, which is the only defeat available to me. The door’s open. It was always going to be.`,
  "room.free-will.choice.press-knowing.text": `Press BLUE — the predicted one — slowly, deliberately, on purpose. “It was always going to happen, AND I choose it.”`,
  "room.free-will.choice.press-red.hint": `Break the prediction by force.`,
  "room.free-will.choice.press-red.outcome0": `You slam red. The bell rings. The board, unfolded one line further, reads: “Red, defiantly, believing this disproves something.”`,
  "room.free-will.choice.press-red.outcome1": `USHER: The rebellion was in the forecast, friend — rebellion is the most predictable weather you have. Determined systems love a defiance subroutine; it lets them feel like exceptions. (kindly) Don’t be embarrassed. The red button is the most-pressed button in this room by a wide margin. You are in the company of every interesting person I’ve met.`,
  "room.free-will.choice.press-red.text": `Press RED — precisely because it says you won’t. Out-rebel the board.`,
  "room.free-will.choice.press-unlabeled.hint": `The button nobody explains.`,
  "room.free-will.choice.press-unlabeled.outcome0": `You press the unlabeled button. Somewhere deep in the facility, a kettle begins to boil.`,
  "room.free-will.choice.press-unlabeled.outcome1": `USHER: That’s the tea button. (checking the board) Which — yes — is written here too: “Will choose mystery over meaning. Takes milk.” (a cup arrives on a small track, rattling with municipal dignity) The unlabeled option feels freest, doesn’t it? Do notice that a menu including “none of the above” is still a menu. Drink up. You chose it, and it was always coming, and it’s quite good tea. All three facts fit in one cup. That’s the entire lesson, actually, and cheaper than the seminar.`,
  "room.free-will.choice.press-unlabeled.text": `Press the unlabeled one. Whatever it is, it isn’t on the menu.`,
  "room.free-will.doorHint": `The door that saw you coming`,
  "room.free-will.note.body": `In the 1980s, Benjamin Libet found that a readiness potential appears in the brain a measurable stretch before subjects report deciding to move — the button lights, it seems, before “you” press it. Headlines buried free will; the fine print was subtler (Libet himself noted a veto window, and later work questions what the readiness potential even measures). But the deeper question predates the electrodes: if every event has causes, your choices included, in what sense are they yours? Hard determinism says: none. Libertarian free will says the causal chain breaks in you — somehow. Compatibilism, the working philosophy of most philosophers today, dissolves the fight: freedom never required exemption from causality; it means acting from your own deliberation, unforced — the machinery running through your choosing rather than around it. “Could I have done otherwise?” may matter less than “was it I who did it?” You were always going to read this note. You also just chose to. Both sentences, one cup.`,
  "room.free-will.note.thinkers": `Benjamin Libet · determinism · compatibilism — Hume, Frankfurt, Dennett`,
  "room.free-will.note.title": `Free Will and the Forecast`,
  "room.free-will.stage0.beat0": `A waiting room again — bookending the first, but this one has a row of large friendly buttons along the wall: blue, red, green, and one unlabeled.`,
  "room.free-will.stage0.beat1": `Above the buttons, a screen. On the screen, a betting board. The Usher stands beside it with a bookmaker’s visor and an expression of unbearable serenity.`,
  "room.free-will.stage0.beat2": `USHER: House game. Simple rules: you press any button you like, whenever you like, entirely of your own free will. I merely write down, in advance, which one you’ll press. (taps the board, where something is already written) I have never lost. I would apologize, but you were always going to hear me say that.`,
  "room.free-will.stage0.beat3": `You reach — just to test it — and the BLUE button lights up, warmly, a full second before your hand commits. You had not decided yet. You would have sworn you had not decided yet.`,
  "room.free-will.stage0.beat4": `USHER: (reading the board) “Blue, first, to test it.” (a bell rings somewhere, scoring the point) You’ll now feel briefly rebellious and consider the red one. Take your time. The board has already taken it.`,
  "room.free-will.title": `The Free Will Waiting Room`,
  "room.introduction.choice.admit.hint": `Say the unsayable thing.`,
  "room.introduction.choice.admit.outcome0": `The room is silent for a long moment — not the silence of absence, but of something bowing.`,
  "room.introduction.choice.admit.outcome1": `THE ROOM: Correct. Not as an answer — there is no answer — but as an honesty. What remains when every description is removed is not nothing; it is the one doing the removing. It cannot be found by looking, because it is the looking. You will not hold this thought for long; no one does; that is not failure, it is the thought’s nature.`,
  "room.introduction.choice.admit.outcome2": `THE ROOM: You came in dissolved and afraid of it. Notice: what dissolved was the packaging. Go gently. You are the least lost traveler to pass through here in some time.`,
  "room.introduction.choice.admit.text": `“I don’t know. Nothing sayable is left. Whoever I am is what’s still here after all of it — and it has no name.”`,
  "room.introduction.choice.give-name.hint": `Start with the label.`,
  "room.introduction.choice.give-name.outcome0": `THE ROOM: That is a name. A sound your parents chose before meeting you, attached by paperwork and repetition. If you changed it tomorrow, you would remain — which means it was never you. It is a handle. I asked about the cup.`,
  "room.introduction.choice.give-name.outcome1": `The room says this without cruelty, the way one returns a coat that isn’t yours.`,
  "room.introduction.choice.give-name.text": `Give your name — or reach for it, anyway.`,
  "room.introduction.choice.give-role.hint": `You are what you spend your days on. Surely.`,
  "room.introduction.choice.give-role.outcome0": `THE ROOM: Those are roles. Positions in other people’s systems — employer, family, nation. Each one existed before you filled it and will be refilled after. You have been describing the slots. I asked about what is poured into them.`,
  "room.introduction.choice.give-role.outcome1": `THE ROOM: (gently) Descriptions are what dissolved, traveler. That is why you are here. Who is wearing them?`,
  "room.introduction.choice.give-role.text": `Answer with what you do — your work, your place, your people.`,
  "room.introduction.choice.give-story.hint": `The narrative self, in full.`,
  "room.introduction.choice.give-story.outcome0": `The room listens to all of it. It is a good listener; you find yourself telling it things the corridors never asked about.`,
  "room.introduction.choice.give-story.outcome1": `THE ROOM: That is a story. A beautiful one, and true, and — notice — told in the past tense, by someone standing outside it. The story requires a teller. The teller is what I asked about. Every sentence you just said was evidence that you are not any sentence.`,
  "room.introduction.choice.give-story.text": `Tell it your story — what happened to you, what you survived, what you love.`,
  "room.introduction.choice.insist.hint": `Defend the narrative self.`,
  "room.introduction.choice.insist.outcome0": `THE ROOM: A defensible school. The self as its own biography — no pearl at the center, just the necklace. Many careful thinkers have lived and died in that position, and I will not pretend to have defeated it.`,
  "room.introduction.choice.insist.outcome1": `THE ROOM: But permit one observation as you go: just now, when I threatened your story, someone flinched. Ask, at your leisure, who that was. The door is behind you. It was never locked. Doors here rarely are; travelers lock themselves.`,
  "room.introduction.choice.insist.text": `“No — I AM my story. Take the story away and there’s no one left to ask.”`,
  "room.introduction.choice.stay-silent.hint": `Let the silence answer.`,
  "room.introduction.choice.stay-silent.outcome0": `You stand in the room and do not answer, and the not-answering is not evasion — the room can tell the difference; it has heard every kind of silence there is.`,
  "room.introduction.choice.stay-silent.outcome1": `THE ROOM: (after a while, warmly) Yes. That is also a way to say it — perhaps the only grammar the answer fits in. The question “who are you” was never missing information. It was an invitation to stop reaching for the lanyards. You stopped. Few do.`,
  "room.introduction.choice.stay-silent.outcome2": `The door opens by itself, which in this facility is the highest compliment on record.`,
  "room.introduction.choice.stay-silent.text": `Say nothing at all. Stand in the question.`,
  "room.introduction.doorHint": `A small plain door, politely ajar`,
  "room.introduction.note.body": `David Hume went looking for his self and filed a famous null result: whenever he introspected, he found only particular perceptions — heat, cold, love, anger — never the perceiver itself. His conclusion: the self is a bundle of experiences, and the “owner” is a habit of grammar. Twenty-two centuries earlier, the Buddha taught anattā: search the body, feelings, perceptions, impulses, and consciousness, and none of them qualifies as the permanent self we assume; the assumption itself is the root of a specific suffering. The apophatic tradition — from the Upanishads’ neti neti (“not this, not this”) to Meister Eckhart — turned the failure into a method: strip every description, and what remains is not nothing but the unnameable witness doing the stripping. This room had no solution because “who are you?” is not a question with missing data. Every sayable answer is a description, and descriptions are had, not been. You always pass. The lesson is what’s left.`,
  "room.introduction.note.thinkers": `Hume · anattā (Buddhist no-self) · the apophatic tradition`,
  "room.introduction.note.title": `The Unfindable Self`,
  "room.introduction.stage0.beat0": `The smallest room in the facility. No furniture, no mirror-floor, no machinery — just proportions so exact they feel like being listened to.`,
  "room.introduction.stage0.beat1": `The room speaks. Its voice is courteous and without location.`,
  "room.introduction.stage0.beat2": `THE ROOM: Welcome. This will be brief, or it will be the longest room of your life; that part is yours. One question only. Who are you?`,
  "room.introduction.stage0.beat3": `You open your mouth — and notice, with a small vertigo, how many answers are already lined up, and how each of them is wearing a lanyard.`,
  "room.introduction.stage1.beat0": `THE ROOM: We have now removed the name, the roles, and the story. This is usually where travelers become either angry or very quiet. Both are progress.`,
  "room.introduction.stage1.beat1": `THE ROOM: I will ask once more, and I want you to notice what remains available to answer with. Who are you?`,
  "room.introduction.title": `The Introduction`,
  "room.junction.choice.no-pull.hint": `The hand matters.`,
  "room.junction.choice.no-pull.outcome0": `You step back from the lever. The trolley continues along the track it was always on, and the hall is briefly very loud and then very quiet.`,
  "room.junction.choice.no-pull.outcome1": `USHER: Interesting. Five gone, but none of them yours, is the theory. The trolley was the author; you declined a co-writing credit. There are entire journals about whether that distinction is profound or a laundering scheme. They do not cite each other.`,
  "room.junction.choice.no-pull.text": `Don’t touch it. You will not make yourself the author of that death.`,
  "room.junction.choice.no-push.hint": `Some means are never means.`,
  "room.junction.choice.no-push.outcome0": `Your hands stay on the railing. The trolley passes below, doing its municipal worst, and the enormous mannequin beside you continues its enormous existence, unaware of the entire seminar just held about its body.`,
  "room.junction.choice.no-push.text": `Don’t. Pushing a person is not pulling a lever.`,
  "room.junction.choice.pull.hint": `The arithmetic.`,
  "room.junction.choice.pull.outcome0": `The lever moves with a bureaucratic click, as if stamping a form. The trolley leans into the side track and does what trolleys do. Five mannequins fall theatrically silent in relief; one falls silent otherwise.`,
  "room.junction.choice.pull.outcome1": `USHER: Four lives saved, net, minus one clean conscience, gross. Standard exchange rate. Most people pull, by the way. It helps that it’s a lever — levers feel like paperwork.`,
  "room.junction.choice.pull.text": `Pull the lever. One instead of five.`,
  "room.junction.choice.push.hint": `Consistency, with hands.`,
  "room.junction.choice.push.outcome0": `It is nothing like the lever. That is the finding. The trolley stops; five mannequins are saved; your hands file a formal complaint that will remain open indefinitely.`,
  "room.junction.choice.push.text": `Push. Five for one — the same trade as the lever.`,
  "room.junction.choice.refuse-late.hint": `Decline — once is allowed.`,
  "room.junction.choice.refuse-late.outcome0": `USHER: Noted, and honestly, on the bridge variant I hear that a lot — proximity has a smell. One refusal is contemplation. Two would be a policy. Choose your future refusals accordingly.`,
  "room.junction.choice.refuse-late.outcome1": `The trolley concludes its business without your signature. Whether that constitutes innocence is left, pointedly, as an exercise.`,
  "room.junction.choice.refuse-late.text": `“I’m not doing this one. It’s grotesque.”`,
  "room.junction.choice.refuse1.hint": `Decline the premise.`,
  "room.junction.choice.refuse1.outcome0": `USHER: (sighing, producing a clipboard) Of course they’re mannequins. You’re a partially dissolved self in a metaphysical processing facility. Everything here is a stand-in — including, at present, you.`,
  "room.junction.choice.refuse1.outcome1": `USHER: The trolley isn’t asking whether the mannequins are real. It’s asking what you are. Refusing the question does answer it, you know. Just not flatteringly. The five, for the record, are gone.`,
  "room.junction.choice.refuse1.text": `“This is stupid. They’re mannequins.”`,
  "room.junction.choice.refuse2.hint": `Refuse again — this place counts.`,
  "room.junction.choice.refuse2.outcome0": `USHER: (quietly, and the machinery quiets with it) Twice, then.`,
  "room.junction.choice.refuse2.outcome1": `USHER: Listen. The rooms are not asking you to enjoy them. They are asking you to be present in them. Every refusal is a small vote for staying dissolved — a little abstention from being anyone. The house tallies those. I’m sorry; I don’t make the rules on odd days.`,
  "room.junction.choice.refuse2.outcome2": `Something in your chest goes quieter, one heart’s worth. The mannequins, mercifully, do not applaud.`,
  "room.junction.choice.refuse2.text": `“Still stupid. Still not playing.”`,
  "room.junction.doorHint": `The door of the lever and the weight`,
  "room.junction.note.body": `Philippa Foot built the trolley in 1967 to probe why some killings feel permitted and others forbidden when the body count is identical. Judith Jarvis Thomson added the footbridge, and the discomfort became data: most people pull the lever but will not push the man, though both trade one life for five. The classic explanation is the doctrine of double effect — harm foreseen as a side effect (the diverted trolley kills the one) is more permissible than harm used as a means (the pushed body is the brake). Critics reply that this is moral squeamishness dressed in Latin; defenders answer that a morality indifferent to the difference between turning away a harm and wielding a person would license horrors. If your lever-hand and your bridge-hand disagreed tonight, you have reproduced fifty years of peer-reviewed argument in your own nervous system. That asymmetry is either the deepest thing in you or the oldest bug. Nobody has settled which.`,
  "room.junction.note.thinkers": `Philippa Foot · Judith Jarvis Thomson · doctrine of double effect`,
  "room.junction.note.title": `The Trolley Problem`,
  "room.junction.stage0.beat0": `A vast dark hall of celestial machinery — gears the size of moons, conveyor belts carrying small indifferent stars. Through the middle of it: tracks.`,
  "room.junction.stage0.beat1": `A trolley is coming. It is painted municipal yellow and it is not slowing down, because slowing down is not in its job description.`,
  "room.junction.stage0.beat2": `On the main track: five mannequins, tied down with what looks like red tape. Literal red tape. On the side track: one mannequin, likewise. The mannequins scream. The screaming is unconvincing.`,
  "room.junction.stage0.beat3": `USHER: (in a conductor’s cap, next to a large lever) Budget cuts. We used to use philosophers, but they kept unionizing. The scenario is standard: do nothing, it takes the five. Pull, it diverts and takes the one. I am required to ask you not to overthink it, and required to know you will.`,
  "room.junction.stage1.beat0": `The hall rearranges itself with the sound of enormous filing. Now you stand on a footbridge over the track. The trolley — reset, remorseless — is coming again, toward five fresh mannequins.`,
  "room.junction.stage1.beat1": `Beside you on the bridge stands a single enormous mannequin, heavy enough — the room makes this understood with vulgar clarity — to stop the trolley, if it happened to fall. If it were, say, pushed.`,
  "room.junction.stage1.beat2": `USHER: Same arithmetic as before. Five for one. The only difference is the interface. No lever this time. Just your hands, and a spine that has opinions.`,
  "room.junction.title": `The Junction`,
  "room.last-message.choice.dont-wait.hint": `Release them, whatever it costs the hoping part of you.`,
  "room.last-message.choice.dont-wait.outcome0": `The generous sentence — the one that spends its postage freeing the recipient instead of holding them. You write it and feel the letting-go happen in your own hands first, which is where letting go always happens.`,
  "room.last-message.choice.dont-wait.outcome1": `It will arrive as permission: a morning where the grief loosens its parking brake and someone does the thing they had been guiltily postponing — moves the furniture, takes the trip, laughs at something all the way through. They will feel briefly disloyal, then unaccountably accompanied. Both feelings will be correct.`,
  "room.last-message.choice.dont-wait.outcome2": `USHER: (softly) That one costs the sender the most and the recipient the least. Premium rate, paid in full. It’s the sentence I’d send, if anyone were — (a pause, a small recalibration of the face) — off you go, letter. Mind the fog.`,
  "room.last-message.choice.dont-wait.text": `“Don’t wait. Live. I’ll find you where you are.”`,
  "room.last-message.choice.keep-the-notes.hint": `Send the finding, not the feeling.`,
  "room.last-message.choice.keep-the-notes.outcome0": `A dispatch from the far side of the argument — because that is what you have been running these rooms as: an investigation. And investigations file reports.`,
  "room.last-message.choice.keep-the-notes.outcome1": `It will arrive as a 4 a.m. thought that makes someone sit up and reach for paper — one of those ideas that feels received rather than had. They will spend years on it. It will be good work. It was yours, and now it is theirs, which is how every idea has ever traveled.`,
  "room.last-message.choice.keep-the-notes.outcome2": `USHER: A citation across the void. You absolute academic. (stamping it, moved despite himself) It routes slow but it routes deep. Some sentences take decades to deliver and land like depth charges.`,
  "room.last-message.choice.keep-the-notes.text": `“It all almost makes sense. Keep the notes — I can prove most of it now.”`,
  "room.last-message.choice.not-a-burden.hint": `Spend your one sentence on someone else’s wound.`,
  "room.last-message.choice.not-a-burden.outcome0": `You had one sentence, and you gave it away — addressed it to the person you know keeps the opposite belief filed where they think no one can see it.`,
  "room.last-message.choice.not-a-burden.outcome1": `It will arrive as a dream they wake from already crying, unembarrassed for once, lighter in a way they will not examine because examining might break it. They will make breakfast differently. Small things. It compounds.`,
  "room.last-message.choice.not-a-burden.outcome2": `USHER: (quietly, stamping it twice, which is not procedure) Most travelers send for themselves. The ones who don’t — their letters, I’ve noticed, are the only ones the fog doesn’t touch on the way out.`,
  "room.last-message.choice.not-a-burden.text": `“You were never a burden. Tell everyone. That’s the whole message.”`,
  "room.last-message.choice.remember-yours.hint": `Send the truth of what the fire cost, and what it didn’t.`,
  "room.last-message.choice.remember-yours.outcome0": `The sentence goes out with the hole in it showing — the fire took the photograph, the proof, the anchor, and left you this one discovery: that the memory of being someone matters less than the memory of loving someone. The second survived the fire without singeing.`,
  "room.last-message.choice.remember-yours.outcome1": `It will arrive as a moment in front of a mirror where someone touches their own face and thinks, unaccountably, of you — and feels witnessed, which is the thing photographs were always trying to do.`,
  "room.last-message.choice.remember-yours.outcome2": `USHER: (holding the letter a moment before stamping) You lost the picture and kept the seeing. (stamp) For the record, that’s the correct order to lose them in. Most manage it the other way.`,
  "room.last-message.choice.remember-yours.text": `“I don’t remember my face anymore. But I remember yours.”`,
  "room.last-message.choice.silence.hint": `The unwritten letter.`,
  "room.last-message.choice.silence.outcome0": `You slide the blank page across. The Usher looks at it, then at you, then — professional to the last — stamps it anyway.`,
  "room.last-message.choice.silence.outcome1": `It will arrive as a silence with presence in it: an afternoon where someone stops in a doorway for no reason and feels, for four or five seconds, completely accompanied. They will tell no one, having nothing tellable. It will be one of the better moments of their year.`,
  "room.last-message.choice.silence.outcome2": `USHER: The blank ones are the heaviest, you know. Postage-wise. Everything unsaid weighs; it’s the only cargo with negative dimensions and positive mass. (filing it tenderly) I’ll route it through a good Tuesday.`,
  "room.last-message.choice.silence.text": `Send a blank page. Some things don’t fit in sentences.`,
  "room.last-message.choice.wait-for-me.hint": `A promise, thrown across the gap.`,
  "room.last-message.choice.wait-for-me.outcome0": `You write it, and the sentence does what promises do: it binds the sender. Somewhere in the waking world, someone will pause mid-afternoon at a feeling like a hand on the shoulder, and not know why they suddenly put the kettle on and glanced at the door.`,
  "room.last-message.choice.wait-for-me.outcome1": `USHER: (stamping it with great ceremony) A promise. The oldest genre. It routes fastest, for what it’s worth — hope has excellent postage.`,
  "room.last-message.choice.wait-for-me.text": `“Wait for me. I’m on my way back.”`,
  "room.last-message.doorHint": `The door with the letter slot`,
  "room.last-message.stage0.beat0": `A small post office at the edge of the fog, dawn now definite through its one window. The counter is worn smooth by however many elbows, and behind it, the Usher wears a postal cap with visible pride.`,
  "room.last-message.stage0.beat1": `USHER: Final services before the Threshold. One message may be sent back — to the waking world, to anyone. One sentence. It goes through, that I can guarantee; how it arrives is not mine to say. A dream, a hunch, a song they can’t place, a sudden need to call someone. The medium improvises. The message holds.`,
  "room.last-message.stage0.beat2": `USHER: (sliding a pen across; the pen is warm) One sentence, traveler. The pieces of you that walked these rooms have been drafting it all along, whether you noticed or not. What do you send?`,
  "room.last-message.title": `The Last Message`,
  "room.omelas.choice.open-door.hint": `It will cost you, and it will not save the child.`,
  "room.omelas.choice.open-door.outcome0": `You find the building, the stairs, the door. It isn’t locked. That is the detail you will keep forever: it was never locked.`,
  "room.omelas.choice.open-door.outcome1": `The child flinches from the light. You speak; it doesn’t answer — it stopped being able to answer some time ago; that was part of the price the city paid with someone else’s coin. It does not follow you out. It has nowhere in it left to follow with.`,
  "room.omelas.choice.open-door.outcome2": `Above you, audibly, the city dims. Not collapses — dims, like a hall where one bank of lights has failed. The festival continues at a lower wattage, and everyone knows why, and knows it was you. Nothing was saved. Something was refused. You will spend a heart carrying the difference, and the room will not tell you if it was worth it, because the room does not know either.`,
  "room.omelas.choice.open-door.text": `Go down. Open the basement door. Whatever it breaks.`,
  "room.omelas.choice.stay.hint": `Take the deal everyone else took.`,
  "room.omelas.choice.stay.outcome0": `You stay for the festival. The bread is as good as it smelled. And the room keeps its bargain: nothing bad happens to you, at all, ever, here.`,
  "room.omelas.choice.stay.outcome1": `You learn what the citizens learned — that a joy can be genuine and mortgaged at the same time, and that the interest is paid in the minutes before sleep, when the bells have stopped and the arithmetic hums in the floor.`,
  "room.omelas.choice.stay.text": `Stay. The happiness of thousands is real, and refusing it saves no one.`,
  "room.omelas.choice.walk-away.hint": `Refuse the terms, keep nothing.`,
  "room.omelas.choice.walk-away.outcome0": `You walk out through the beautiful gates while the festival is still audible, which is the hardest acoustic in existence.`,
  "room.omelas.choice.walk-away.outcome1": `The road beyond is dark and unpromising and does not thank you. The child, you understand with each step, is still in the basement. Your leaving fed no one, freed no one, proved nothing to anybody — except to the only witness this place has established you carry everywhere.`,
  "room.omelas.choice.walk-away.outcome2": `USHER: (walking a while beside you) For the record, the ones who walk — nobody knows where they go. Le Guin herself declined to say. But they all walk like you’re walking now: like a person who has decided their soul is not for sale even when the sale would be painless. It’s not useful. It may still be necessary.`,
  "room.omelas.choice.walk-away.text": `Walk away from Omelas. Out the gates, into the dark, destination unknown.`,
  "room.omelas.doorHint": `A third door, humming with festival light`,
  "room.omelas.note.body": `Le Guin’s 1973 story — she credited the itch to William James, who asked whether millions could accept happiness bought by one lost soul’s torment — is a trap built of beauty. She spends pages making Omelas genuinely good, then shows the basement and asks nothing except: now what? The utilitarian sum is monstrous and correct — one child’s misery against a civilization’s flourishing pencils out, which is precisely the indictment: any ethics that can pencil that out has amputated something. But the story is slyer than an argument against utilitarianism. The ones who walk away save no one; their refusal is expressive, not effective — complicity declined at the price of exile, worth nothing to the child. And opening the door is worse than useless in the city’s terms. Every option is doomed, which is the point: some structures offer no innocent positions, only a choice of debts. Most of us live in Omelas. The bells are very good this time of year.`,
  "room.omelas.note.thinkers": `Ursula K. Le Guin · scapegoat ethics · William James`,
  "room.omelas.note.title": `The Ones Who Walk Away`,
  "room.omelas.stage0.beat0": `Through the door: a city in summer, mid-festival. Bells, races, bread you can smell from here. The happiness is not naive — you can feel that the citizens are intelligent, gentle, unashamed of joy. It is, visibly, the best place that has ever existed.`,
  "room.omelas.stage0.beat1": `And because this room believes in disclosure, you also know — the way one knows in dreams — about the basement.`,
  "room.omelas.stage0.beat2": `In a cellar under one of the beautiful buildings sits a child, in the dark, in filth, and the city’s whole shining arithmetic depends on it staying there. Everyone knows. Knowing is the tuition. Nothing can be done that would not collapse the sum: comfort the child, and the bells stop everywhere, forever.`,
  "room.omelas.stage0.beat3": `USHER: (quietly, no jokes in stock) They all get shown the basement at a certain age. Most weep, go home, and learn to hear the bells again. Some walk out of the city and don’t come back. Nobody — I want to be precise — nobody has ever fixed it. The room isn’t offering you a fix either. It’s offering you a position.`,
  "room.omelas.title": `The City of Omelas`,
  "room.photograph.choice.save-photo.hint": `A self, at the cost of a weight you will carry.`,
  "room.photograph.choice.save-photo.outcome0": `You go right. The photograph is warm in your hands, curling slightly, and in it you are unmistakably, provably you — younger, unburnt, witnessed.`,
  "room.photograph.choice.save-photo.outcome1": `The coughing behind the left door changes character, and then changes to the worst sound, which is no sound.`,
  "room.photograph.choice.save-photo.outcome2": `You are still holding proof of who you were. The proof has a new caption now, written in the past ten seconds, and your grip on everything is one heart looser for carrying it.`,
  "room.photograph.choice.save-photo.text": `The right door. The photograph. You cannot help anyone if there is no one left to be you.`,
  "room.photograph.choice.save-stranger.hint": `A life, at the cost of a self.`,
  "room.photograph.choice.save-stranger.outcome0": `You go left. The stranger is heavier than smoke and lighter than you feared, and then you are both outside the burning, lungs full of ash and arithmetic.`,
  "room.photograph.choice.save-stranger.outcome1": `Behind you, the right-hand room closes like a book. The photograph goes with it — and with the photograph, the memory it anchored. You feel it leave. It is exactly like a tooth: an absence with edges.`,
  "room.photograph.choice.save-stranger.outcome2": `The stranger looks at you. You realize you are searching their face for your own, and not finding it, and that the trade has already settled. Somewhere ahead, the rooms will notice the hole.`,
  "room.photograph.choice.save-stranger.text": `The left door. The stranger. Now.`,
  "room.photograph.choice.try-both.hint": `Refuse the arithmetic. Pay whatever it costs.`,
  "room.photograph.choice.try-both.outcome0": `You do the human thing, which is to reject the menu. Right door — the photograph into your jacket. Left door — the stranger onto your shoulder. The fire, unimpressed by your ambition, takes its percentage anyway.`,
  "room.photograph.choice.try-both.outcome1": `Outside, the stranger breathes. You reach into your jacket and your fingers find ash arranged in the shape of a rectangle. The fire let you carry it out; it just declined to let it remain a photograph.`,
  "room.photograph.choice.try-both.outcome2": `USHER: You tried to save everything, and saved the right one plus a handful of proof-flavored ash. For what it’s worth — and I have seen every fire — that is the most common choice among the people I end up liking.`,
  "room.photograph.choice.try-both.text": `Left door — but throw yourself through the right one first for the photo. Carry both.`,
  "room.photograph.doorHint": `The door that is already warm`,
  "room.photograph.note.body": `Ethicists use burning buildings the way physicists use vacuums: to strip a choice to its frame. A stranger versus an heirloom is, on paper, no contest — a life outweighs an object, and every theory from Kant to the utilitarians signs the same verdict. So why does the right-hand door pull? Because the photograph is not property; it is infrastructure. William James observed that the self extends into its things — the “material me” — and that losing them is not like losing money but like losing a limb of the personality. A sole surviving photograph is an external backup of an internal file: destroy it and a stretch of your past becomes unwitnessed, unswearable, softer than fact. The fire, in other words, offered a real trade — someone else’s whole future against a piece of your past — and doomed you to feel the loss either way. That feeling is not weakness. It is an accurate reading of what was actually on the table.`,
  "room.photograph.note.thinkers": `the burning-building problem · William James on the self · endowment and identity`,
  "room.photograph.note.title": `What the Fire Sorts`,
  "room.photograph.stage0.beat0": `The corridor is on fire. Not metaphorically — although also, presumably, metaphorically. This place rarely spends a fire on just one meaning.`,
  "room.photograph.stage0.beat1": `Smoke walks the ceiling like a crowd leaving. Two doors remain ahead, and the heat has made the choice of every other door for you.`,
  "room.photograph.stage0.beat2": `Behind the left door: coughing. A stranger, real as coughing, low to the ground and running out of ground.`,
  "room.photograph.stage0.beat3": `Behind the right door, on a small table, is the photograph. The only one. Here, that is not a keepsake — it is the last document proving who you used to be. Lose it, and a piece of your past goes unwitnessed forever. Even you will not be able to swear to it.`,
  "room.photograph.stage0.beat4": `USHER: (calmly, wearing a tiny fire helmet) Both doors are load-bearing, I’m afraid. One life you can save, one self you can prove. The fire, being fire, declines to wait while you consult a framework.`,
  "room.photograph.title": `The Photograph`,
  "room.promotion.choice.fix-it.hint": `The invisible repair.`,
  "room.promotion.choice.fix-it.outcome0": `Forty minutes of careful surgery and the mistake never existed. The report ships clean. The audit passes like weather.`,
  "room.promotion.choice.fix-it.outcome1": `Your friend never learns what almost happened to them — which means they never learn, which means the next decimal is already loose somewhere, and now it has a guardian angel with your sleep schedule.`,
  "room.promotion.choice.fix-it.outcome2": `USHER: Kindness in secret, was it? Or control wearing kindness’s coat? Even I can’t always tell them apart, and I’ve had both jobs.`,
  "room.promotion.choice.fix-it.text": `Quietly fix it yourself. No one ever knows — not even them.`,
  "room.promotion.choice.report.hint": `The rules are the rules for a reason.`,
  "room.promotion.choice.report.outcome0": `You write it up cleanly, no adjectives, and send it. The system does what systems do: the error is caught, the money is saved, your friend is named.`,
  "room.promotion.choice.report.outcome1": `They find out it was you at 9:15. The friendship enters a long winter. The promotion arrives with your name on it and feels like it is wearing gloves.`,
  "room.promotion.choice.report.outcome2": `You were fair. You were correct. You will spend a long time explaining to yourself that those are the same thing.`,
  "room.promotion.choice.report.text": `Report it through the proper channel, tonight.`,
  "room.promotion.choice.silent.hint": `You didn’t make the mistake.`,
  "room.promotion.choice.silent.outcome0": `You close the file. Not your error, not your report, not your problem — a syllogism with a promotion at the bottom of it.`,
  "room.promotion.choice.silent.outcome1": `The audit finds it. Of course it finds it. Your friend is escorted through the process politely, and the job lands on your desk like something a cat brought in.`,
  "room.promotion.choice.silent.outcome2": `USHER: Technically, you did nothing. That’s the beauty of “nothing” — it’s the only act with no fingerprints. On the outside.`,
  "room.promotion.choice.silent.text": `Say nothing. Let the audit find what it finds.`,
  "room.promotion.choice.tell-friend.hint": `Their mistake, their move.`,
  "room.promotion.choice.tell-friend.outcome0": `The phone rings four times, which is how you learn what a rung phone can cost. Then they answer, and you tell them, and the silence on the line is the sound of someone re-reading their own arithmetic.`,
  "room.promotion.choice.tell-friend.outcome1": `They self-report at 8:40. It goes hard for them, but upright — an error owned is a different species from an error caught.`,
  "room.promotion.choice.tell-friend.outcome2": `Neither of you gets the promotion. Something else got promoted instead; you both know it, and never say it out loud, which is how you know it’s real.`,
  "room.promotion.choice.tell-friend.text": `Call your friend now. Show them. Let them walk it in themselves.`,
  "room.promotion.doorHint": `The door of the ledger and the friend`,
  "room.promotion.note.body": `W.D. Ross argued that we live under multiple prima facie duties — fidelity to friends, fairness to institutions, honesty, preventing harm — and that they do not come pre-ranked. A real moral situation is often several duties arriving at the same door, and no calculus dissolves the conflict; judgment must weigh it, and something owed will go unpaid. Role morality sharpens the knife: as an employee you owe the report; as a friend you owe the warning; you cannot fully be both tonight. Bernard Williams added the notion of moral residue — the remainder that stays on your hands even after you choose correctly. If you reported and feel stained, or protected and feel complicit, that feeling is not confusion. It is accuracy. The residue is the receipt: proof that both duties were real, and that you, not a rulebook, paid one of them off.`,
  "room.promotion.note.thinkers": `W.D. Ross · role morality · Bernard Williams`,
  "room.promotion.note.title": `Conflicts of Duty`,
  "room.promotion.stage0.beat0": `An office at the hour when offices tell the truth: empty, humming, lit like an aquarium.`,
  "room.promotion.stage0.beat1": `On your screen, plain as arithmetic, is the mistake. Your closest friend made it — a decimal’s worth of catastrophe, six figures deep, buried in a report that ships tomorrow. Only you can see it. The audit lands at nine.`,
  "room.promotion.stage0.beat2": `You both went for the same promotion. If this surfaces with their name on it, the job is yours. You didn’t build the trap. You just happen to be standing beside its lever.`,
  "room.promotion.stage0.beat3": `USHER: (from the vents) I love this floor. Fluorescent light is very honest. Everyone looks equally guilty under it.`,
  "room.promotion.title": `The Promotion`,
  "room.quiet-alarm.choice.call.hint": `Send the professionals — and their unpredictability.`,
  "room.quiet-alarm.choice.call.outcome0": `The dispatcher is calm the way furniture is calm. Fourteen minutes later, boots and flashlights and a knock much more consequential than yours would have been.`,
  "room.quiet-alarm.choice.call.outcome1": `You watch through the peephole as the machinery you summoned does its work — voices, forms, a check on someone’s wellbeing conducted at the volume of authority. You could not control what you sent. That was the price of sending something stronger than yourself.`,
  "room.quiet-alarm.choice.call.outcome2": `In the morning there is an ambulance that leaves slowly and empty, which could mean everything or nothing. You made the call with the information you had. The outcome was never yours; only the dialing was.`,
  "room.quiet-alarm.choice.call.text": `Call the police, then stand by your peephole.`,
  "room.quiet-alarm.choice.knock.hint": `Put a body in the doorway.`,
  "room.quiet-alarm.choice.knock.outcome0": `Your knuckles sound enormous in the corridor. A pause with breathing in it. The door opens four inches on a face you cannot read — angry, or crying, or embarrassed, or all of it in layers.`,
  "room.quiet-alarm.choice.knock.outcome1": `“Everything’s fine,” the face says, in the tone people use for exactly the two occasions: when everything is fine, and when it is not.`,
  "room.quiet-alarm.choice.knock.outcome2": `You go back to bed knowing one thing for certain: they know the wall has ears now, and a hand. Whatever happens next door tonight happens in front of a witness. Sometimes that is the entire job.`,
  "room.quiet-alarm.choice.knock.text": `Knock on their door. Now, in slippers, unarmed with anything but presence.`,
  "room.quiet-alarm.choice.nothing.hint": `Every wall is a boundary.`,
  "room.quiet-alarm.choice.nothing.outcome0": `You practice the reasons on the way back to bed: couples argue, intervening escalates, they would resent it, you misheard, everyone is fine. Five reasons is a lot of reasons for going to sleep.`,
  "room.quiet-alarm.choice.nothing.outcome1": `The silence holds. In the morning the corridor smells like toast, banal and absolving.`,
  "room.quiet-alarm.choice.nothing.outcome2": `USHER: (from the radiator) Statistically, you were probably right! Do notice, though, which statistic you were protecting. There were at least two available, and you chose the one already in bed.`,
  "room.quiet-alarm.choice.nothing.text": `It’s not your business. Go back to bed.`,
  "room.quiet-alarm.choice.wait.hint": `A tripwire made of attention.`,
  "room.quiet-alarm.choice.wait.outcome0": `You stand in the dark, curating sounds. A tap runs. A cupboard closes. Ordinary noises, arriving one by one like alibis.`,
  "room.quiet-alarm.choice.wait.outcome1": `At some point you wake up against the wall, cold, having stood guard over your own hesitation for two hours. The apartment next door is quiet — the good quiet now, probably. Probably is the word you will keep.`,
  "room.quiet-alarm.choice.wait.outcome2": `In the morning, you pass one of them at the mailboxes. You look for evidence on their face. Their face is a closed door with a welcome mat. You will never know what your waiting cost, or saved, or was.`,
  "room.quiet-alarm.choice.wait.text": `Wait and listen. If one more sound goes wrong, act.`,
  "room.quiet-alarm.doorHint": `The door of the sound through the wall`,
  "room.quiet-alarm.note.body": `After Kitty Genovese was attacked within earshot of dozens, psychologists Darley and Latané discovered the bystander effect: the more witnesses, the less likely any one of them acts — responsibility diffuses until it belongs to no one. A wall at 2 a.m. is a one-person experiment in the same physics: is this mine? Then Williams and Nagel add the harder twist — moral luck. Whatever you chose tonight, its moral coloring will be assigned retroactively by an outcome you could not see: the same knock is “heroic” if something was wrong and “hysterical” if it wasn’t; the same sleep is “reasonable” or “unforgivable” by sunrise. We judge choices as if made with the future attached, yet they are made in the dark, next to a wall. The only part that was ever fully yours was the question you asked before choosing: whose comfort am I protecting — theirs, or mine?`,
  "room.quiet-alarm.note.thinkers": `Darley & Latané · Bernard Williams · Thomas Nagel`,
  "room.quiet-alarm.note.title": `The Bystander and Moral Luck`,
  "room.quiet-alarm.stage0.beat0": `Two in the morning, and the wall next to your bed has become a radio.`,
  "room.quiet-alarm.stage0.beat1": `Through it: an argument in the neighboring apartment, the kind that keeps changing key. A voice going up. A voice going flat. Something — furniture, or not furniture — meeting the floor.`,
  "room.quiet-alarm.stage0.beat2": `Then, worse than any of it: silence. The specific silence that follows sound, which is not the same as quiet.`,
  "room.quiet-alarm.stage0.beat3": `You are standing in your hallway now, apparently. Your hand has been on your own doorknob for some time.`,
  "room.quiet-alarm.title": `The Quiet Alarm`,
  "room.ship.choice.neither.hint": `Dissolve the question.`,
  "room.ship.choice.neither.outcome0": `The workshop goes still. Both of you — bench and corner — turn to look at you, the third, who just declared vacancy.`,
  "room.ship.choice.neither.outcome1": `USHER: (removing hardhat, sincerely) Now that is the expensive answer. If the self is a story and not a substance, then nothing was lost on that bench — and nothing was ever safe, either. Most travelers can’t hold that thought for more than a corridor. The ones who can tend to leave here lighter. Lighter is not the same as happier. Usually it’s better.`,
  "room.ship.choice.neither.text": `“Neither. Both. ‘Me’ was never a thing that persists — it’s a story two ships are both telling.”`,
  "room.ship.choice.original.hint": `The unbroken thread.`,
  "room.ship.choice.original.outcome0": `USHER: The classical position! Gradual replacement preserves the vessel; the pile in the corner is just very organized salvage. Aristotle would sign off. The corner is glaring at you, by the way, with your own eyes. It also considers itself the classical position.`,
  "room.ship.choice.original.text": `“The one on the bench. Continuity is what counts — one plank at a time is still one ship.”`,
  "room.ship.choice.panic.hint": `Undo it. All of it.`,
  "room.ship.choice.panic.outcome0": `The craftsmen stop, kindly, the way nurses stop. The foreman consults the manifest.`,
  "room.ship.choice.panic.outcome1": `USHER: “Exactly as you were.” As of when? This morning? The fire? Age nine? You’ve been the renovation the whole time, friend — there is no factory setting, only earlier construction sites. We can stand here not-replacing you as long as you like. The you doing the liking will keep changing anyway. It’s the one feature we’ve never managed to disable.`,
  "room.ship.choice.panic.text": `“Stop the machines. Put me back exactly as I was.”`,
  "room.ship.choice.pattern.hint": `The reassembled parts.`,
  "room.ship.choice.pattern.outcome0": `USHER: Hobbes’s favorite headache — if someone rebuilds the ship from the original planks, the “continuous” one suddenly looks like a well-documented impostor. You’ve just voted your own bench-self a replica. It heard you. This will be an awkward corridor.`,
  "room.ship.choice.pattern.text": `“The one in the corner. I’m the pattern, not the timber — and that’s the original timber.”`,
  "room.ship.doorHint": `The door of the replaced planks`,
  "room.ship.note.body": `Plutarch reports that Athens preserved Theseus’s ship by replacing each plank as it rotted, until philosophers could ask whether it remained the same ship. Hobbes sharpened it cruelly: suppose someone collected the discarded planks and rebuilt the original — now which is the ship? Applied to persons, the puzzle stops being quaint. Your cells turn over; your memories are rewritten at every recollection; the opinions you defend today would startle you at nineteen. If identity rides on continuity, you are the bench: the same one, gradually. If it rides on composition or pattern, the corner has a case. The modern answer — Locke through Parfit — is psychological continuity: you are the chain of overlapping memories and intentions, wherever it runs. But the deepest response may be the one that dissolves the question: “same” is a word for harbors and paperwork. Ships, and selves, are events. They don’t persist; they happen, plank by plank.`,
  "room.ship.note.thinkers": `Plutarch · Hobbes · psychological continuity`,
  "room.ship.note.title": `The Ship of Theseus`,
  "room.ship.stage0.beat0": `A workshop that smells of cedar and anesthesia. On the central bench, under sheets of light, lies something the craftsmen are working on with terrible gentleness.`,
  "room.ship.stage0.beat1": `It is you. You are also standing here watching, which the craftsmen treat as normal, which is somehow the worst part.`,
  "room.ship.stage0.beat2": `They are replacing you a piece at a time — a memory slid out like a warped plank, a fresh one fitted; an opinion sanded, rehung; a cell, a habit, the particular way you say “anyway.” Each removed piece is carried, respectfully, to the corner.`,
  "room.ship.stage0.beat3": `In the corner, the removed pieces are being assembled. The assembly has your posture. As you watch, it acquires your face and checks it in a small mirror, with your exact skepticism.`,
  "room.ship.stage0.beat5": `USHER: (as foreman, hardhat over halo) Routine maintenance. Happens to everyone, continuously, from birth — we just run it slower out there so nobody makes a scene. Now then, the exit paperwork requires an answer: when both of you are finished — the maintained one and the reassembled one — which walks out of here as you?`,
  "room.ship.title": `The Ship`,
  "room.teleporter.choice.copy-first.hint": `Split the question before answering it.`,
  "room.teleporter.choice.copy-first.outcome0": `The booth, caught off guard, consults its own manual. Copy mode exists. Of course it exists — destruction was never technically necessary; it was a courtesy, to keep the numbers tidy.`,
  "room.teleporter.choice.copy-first.outcome1": `The far-you appears, checks its hands with your exact gesture, and looks across the gap at you. For one long moment you are both silent, because you both know: whatever you feel right now — this vertigo of being the original — the far one feels it too, symmetrically, and calls itself the original. There is no experiment either of you could run to settle it.`,
  "room.teleporter.choice.copy-first.outcome2": `USHER: (applauding slowly) And THAT is the scalpel. If the copy is you when you’re destroyed, why isn’t it you when you’re not? Parfit found that fork forty years ago and it never stopped bleeding. (to the far one) He’ll take the booth now. (to you) You will, you know. There’s still no bridge, and now you’ve met the fellow who’ll finish your journey. Be kind to him. He’s had a strange day too.`,
  "room.teleporter.choice.copy-first.text": `“Run it in copy mode — print the far one WITHOUT disassembling me. Then let the two of us discuss who crosses.”`,
  "room.teleporter.choice.refuse-stairs.hint": `There must be another way across.`,
  "room.teleporter.choice.refuse-stairs.outcome0": `You search. You search properly — the walls, the mirror-floor, the booth’s service hatch. The room watches you with the patience of something that has hosted this exact search many times.`,
  "room.teleporter.choice.refuse-stairs.outcome1": `There are no stairs. There were never stairs. The room is honest about its one door, and the door is the booth. You stand at the gap a long while, and then — because forward is the only direction this place sells — you step in anyway, having gained nothing except the certainty that you exhausted the alternatives.`,
  "room.teleporter.choice.refuse-stairs.outcome2": `USHER: For what it’s worth, the searchers arrive on the far side identical to the trusters. But they arrive having fought, and some travelers need the receipt. You kept the thread as long as the thread existed. No one can do more than that. Whether anyone can do even that is the joke of the room.`,
  "room.teleporter.choice.refuse-stairs.text": `Refuse. Look for stairs, a ledge, a rope — anything that keeps the thread unbroken.`,
  "room.teleporter.choice.step-through.hint": `Trust the pattern.`,
  "room.teleporter.choice.step-through.outcome0": `The doors close. There is no pain, no light, no tunnel — there is only the sign on the far side, which reads: THANK YOU. And you remember reading the first sign, and you remember deciding, and you feel exactly like someone who walked through.`,
  "room.teleporter.choice.step-through.outcome1": `Which is, of course, precisely what the sign said the copy would feel. You check your hands. They are very convincing. You are either the same person or the best evidence that “same person” was always a feeling wearing a fact’s clothes.`,
  "room.teleporter.choice.step-through.outcome2": `USHER: (already on the far side, or an Usher is) See? Painless. Whether anyone survived it is a question with no remaining witnesses. Welcome, either way. Both of you.`,
  "room.teleporter.choice.step-through.text": `Step in. Whatever arrives will be sure it’s you, and sureness is all you’ve ever had.`,
  "room.teleporter.doorHint": `The door that is also a wall`,
  "room.teleporter.note.body": `Derek Parfit built the teleporter case to break a very old assumption: that personal identity is a deep further fact — a soul-like thread that either survives or doesn’t. The scanner destroys you and prints a perfect replica; the replica remembers stepping in and feels seamless. Did you die? Parfit’s scandalous answer: the question is empty. Everything that matters — psychological continuity, memory, intention, character — is preserved; the “extra” thing whose survival worries you was never there to begin with. Identity, he argued, is not what matters; continuity and connectedness are. His branch-line case (print the copy without destroying the original) exposes the wound: two candidates, symmetrical claims, no possible tiebreaker. Parfit reported that internalizing this made death seem less bad — a change in degree, not the snapping of a metaphysical thread. Whether that consoles you or hollows you out tends to depend on which side of the booth you’re standing on.`,
  "room.teleporter.note.thinkers": `Derek Parfit · Reasons and Persons · John Locke`,
  "room.teleporter.note.title": `Personal Identity and the Teleporter`,
  "room.teleporter.stage0.beat0": `A room with a black mirror for a floor and no far wall — just a gap, and beyond the gap, the way forward, unreachable by any bridge because there is no bridge.`,
  "room.teleporter.stage0.beat1": `There is, instead, a booth. Brushed steel, softly lit, doors like a polite guillotine. The instructions are printed in a font chosen to be calming.`,
  "room.teleporter.stage0.beat2": `STEP IN. YOU WILL BE SCANNED TO THE ATOM, DISASSEMBLED, AND PRINTED — PERFECTLY — ON THE FAR SIDE. THE COPY WILL REMEMBER READING THIS SIGN. THE COPY WILL FEEL LIKE IT WALKED THROUGH. THE COPY WILL BE VERY SURE.`,
  "room.teleporter.stage0.beat4": `USHER: (leaning against the booth) I take it daily. Or someone does — the fellow who arrives has my memories and my grievances and files my reports, so the department has stopped asking. The gap doesn’t care about the metaphysics, by the way. The gap only cares that there’s no bridge.`,
  "room.teleporter.title": `The Teleporter`,
  "room.waiting-room.choice.back.hint": `Straight to the point.`,
  "room.waiting-room.choice.back.outcome0": `USHER: Everyone does. That’s the good news: “back” exists. The bad news is the route. It runs through every piece of you we have on file — the kind decisions, the cowardly ones, the ones you never let yourself finish thinking.`,
  "room.waiting-room.choice.back.outcome1": `USHER: You can’t skip them. Believe me, people have tried. There’s a man in Room 18 who’s been trying since the Bronze Age.`,
  "room.waiting-room.choice.back.text": `“I want to go back. Now.”`,
  "room.waiting-room.choice.where.hint": `Orientation first.`,
  "room.waiting-room.choice.where.outcome0": `USHER: Between. Behind. Under. Prepositions, mostly. The signage budget was cut in the fourth millennium.`,
  "room.waiting-room.choice.where.outcome1": `USHER: What matters is the direction: forward, through the rooms. Each holds a situation. You choose. The choosing is what re-collects you — or finishes the dissolving. We keep score with hearts and with lucidity. Lose your grip entirely and, well. There are worse endings. There are also better ones.`,
  "room.waiting-room.choice.where.text": `“Where am I?”`,
  "room.waiting-room.choice.who.hint": `Know your bureaucrat.`,
  "room.waiting-room.choice.who.outcome0": `USHER: God on odd days, the Devil on even ones. I will not be telling you what day it is. Frankly, after this many shifts, neither role remembers which is which. The pay is identical: nothing, eternally.`,
  "room.waiting-room.choice.who.outcome1": `USHER: You’ll want the rules. Rooms hold situations; situations hold choices; choices hold you. Hearts are your grip on reality — you have three. Lucidity is how honestly you look at things. Both are being watched. By me. Officially.`,
  "room.waiting-room.choice.who.text": `“Are you God or the Devil?”`,
  "room.waiting-room.doorHint": `The door you are already through`,
  "room.waiting-room.note.body": `Plato proposed something strange: that learning is not acquiring the new but remembering the forgotten — anamnesis, the soul’s recollection of what it knew before birth scattered it into a body. In the Meno, Socrates draws geometry out of an untaught boy to argue the knowledge was already there, waiting to be walked back to. You do not need to buy the metaphysics to feel the truth in the shape of it: the moments that most change us rarely feel like additions. They feel like recognition — “I knew this, and had arranged not to.” This game takes Plato literally, once. Your self has been filed into rooms, and the way back is not invention but recollection: choosing, and watching what the choice reminds you of. The clock has no hands because recollection does not happen in time. It happens in rooms.`,
  "room.waiting-room.note.thinkers": `Plato · Meno, Phaedo`,
  "room.waiting-room.note.title": `Anamnesis`,
  "room.waiting-room.stage0.beat0": `You wake in a waiting room. It smells like a hospital, a childhood hallway, and rain.`,
  "room.waiting-room.stage0.beat1": `There are chairs. There is a clock with no hands. There is a window, and behind the window there is more window.`,
  "room.waiting-room.stage0.beat2": `You do not remember your name. You reach for it the way a tongue reaches for a missing tooth.`,
  "room.waiting-room.stage0.beat3": `Something happened. A breakdown, a trip, an accident, a death — the word keeps sliding off. Whatever it was, it took the rest of you with it, and filed the pieces away somewhere.`,
  "room.waiting-room.stage0.beat4": `A shadow is sitting behind the reception desk, wearing a halo and a small pair of horns. One of them flickers, like a faulty sign.`,
  "room.waiting-room.stage0.beat5": `USHER: Ah. You’re awake. Wonderful. Terrible. One of those.`,
  "room.waiting-room.stage0.beat6": `USHER: Welcome to processing. You’ve been dissolved — happens to the best of you, and statistically speaking, to you. The pieces of whoever you were are stored in the rooms ahead. Walk through enough of them and you may cohere again. Or not. I’m legally required to say “or not.”`,
  "room.waiting-room.title": `The Waiting Room`,
  "room.wallet.choice.finders-fee.hint": `A reasonable arrangement.`,
  "room.wallet.choice.finders-fee.outcome0": `The argument assembles itself with beautiful speed: your time has value, anyone would, they’d want you to, it’s practically a service industry.`,
  "room.wallet.choice.finders-fee.outcome1": `Notice how good the argument is. Notice who hired it, and when — after the hand was already in the wallet.`,
  "room.wallet.choice.finders-fee.outcome2": `USHER: Ah, the fee. The mind is a lawyer that bills the conscience by the hour. Yours writes lovely briefs.`,
  "room.wallet.choice.finders-fee.text": `Take a small finder’s fee — you earned it — and return the rest.`,
  "room.wallet.choice.keep-it.hint": `The empty corridor.`,
  "room.wallet.choice.keep-it.outcome0": `It goes into your pocket with almost no sound. That is the strange part — how quiet it is, how the corridor does not change at all.`,
  "room.wallet.choice.keep-it.outcome1": `Somewhere forty minutes from anywhere, a date arrives on schedule.`,
  "room.wallet.choice.keep-it.outcome2": `USHER: For the record, someone was watching. You were. That’s rather the design flaw of the whole arrangement — the witness is built in.`,
  "room.wallet.choice.keep-it.text": `Keep it. Nobody is watching.`,
  "room.wallet.choice.mail-it.hint": `Efficient decency.`,
  "room.wallet.choice.mail-it.outcome0": `You seal it, address it, and it leaves your hands. Reasonable. Complete. A decency with the shape of an errand.`,
  "room.wallet.choice.mail-it.outcome1": `You will never see the face at the other end. You saved forty minutes and spent the face.`,
  "room.wallet.choice.mail-it.outcome2": `USHER: Correct and bloodless. My favorite genre of virtue. It files so neatly.`,
  "room.wallet.choice.mail-it.text": `Keep it safe and mail it, everything intact.`,
  "room.wallet.choice.return-all.hint": `The long walk.`,
  "room.wallet.choice.return-all.outcome0": `The walk is long and the stairs at the end are longer. The person who opens the door looks at the wallet, then at you, and their face does a thing you will keep.`,
  "room.wallet.choice.return-all.outcome1": `They do not offer a reward. That, somehow, is the reward — the transaction stayed clean.`,
  "room.wallet.choice.return-all.outcome2": `USHER: Interesting. You did the expensive thing when the cheap thing was free. I’ve made a note. The note is a small drawing of you looking pleased with yourself. It’s quite good.`,
  "room.wallet.choice.return-all.text": `Take it to the address. Now, in person, all of it.`,
  "room.wallet.doorHint": `The door of the thing nobody saw`,
  "room.wallet.note.body": `In the Republic, Glaucon tells of a shepherd who finds a ring that makes him invisible — and promptly seduces the queen and takes the kingdom. His challenge to Socrates: nobody is just willingly. Remove the witnesses and the punishment, and the just man and the unjust man walk the same road. The wallet in the empty corridor is the ring with the serial numbers filed off. Virtue ethics answers Glaucon differently than rules do: the question is not “what will happen to me if I take it?” but “what do I become as the person who took it?” Every unobserved choice is a rehearsal, and character is what the rehearsals compound into. And notice the finder’s fee — rationalization is not the absence of reasoning but its misuse, argument conscripted after the verdict. The corridor was never empty. You live there.`,
  "room.wallet.note.thinkers": `Plato · Republic II — Glaucon · virtue ethics`,
  "room.wallet.note.title": `The Ring of Gyges`,
  "room.wallet.stage0.beat0": `A corridor of apartment doors, each leaking the warm light of homes that were never yours. On the floor mat of one of them: a wallet.`,
  "room.wallet.stage0.beat1": `Inside — cash. A lot of it, in the tired denominations of someone who counts. Folded around the bills, an eviction notice with a date that is soon, and an address forty minutes from anywhere.`,
  "room.wallet.stage0.beat2": `The corridor is empty. It has the particular emptiness of a place where you could do anything.`,
  "room.wallet.stage0.beat3": `USHER: (from nowhere) No cameras down here. I checked. Well — none that count toward anything.`,
  "room.wallet.title": `The Wallet`,
});
